window.PQCourseData = {"lessons":{"Variables & Types":"## Names, objects, and identity\nA variable is a name bound to an object, not a box that permanently owns a value. Assignment changes the binding: `x = [1]` then `y = x` means both names refer to the same list. `id()` can help investigate identity during a process, but do not use identity as a substitute for equality. Use `==` for value comparison and `is` for singleton checks such as `value is None`.\n```python\nx = [1, 2]\ny = x\ny.append(3)\nprint(x)          # [1, 2, 3]\nprint(x is y)     # True\nprint(x == [1, 2, 3])\n```\nThis aliasing model explains many bugs: mutating a shared object is visible through every alias. Rebinding a name is different from mutating the object it used to reference.\nCHECK: After `b = a`, what does `b.append(9)` do when `a` and `b` refer to the same list? ;; The shared list changes ;; Only b gets a separate list ;; Python raises an error ;; Assignment creates an alias unless you explicitly copy the list\n\n## Type contracts and boundary checks\nPython is dynamically typed, but robust programs still have type contracts. Decide which inputs are valid, validate at the boundary, and keep internal code simpler. `isinstance(value, int)` also accepts `bool` because `bool` subclasses `int`; when that distinction matters, use `type(value) is int`. Type hints document intent and help static tools, but generally do not enforce types at runtime.\n```python\ndef percentage(part: float, whole: float) -> float:\n    if whole <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"whole must be positive\")\n    return part / whole * 100\n```\nPrefer explicit exceptions over silently inventing fallback values. This makes invalid states visible to callers and tests.\nCHECK: Do annotations automatically reject a string passed to a function annotated with `int`? ;; No, normal Python does not enforce hints at runtime ;; Yes, every call is type-checked ;; Only in functions with return hints ;; Hints are documentation for humans and static tools unless extra runtime checks are added","Numbers & Math":"## Integer arithmetic and exactness\nIntegers have arbitrary precision, so integer calculations do not overflow at a fixed machine-word width in ordinary Python. `/` always produces a floating-point result; `//` rounds down toward negative infinity, not toward zero. `%` is defined consistently with floor division, so negative values can surprise code ported from languages with truncating division.\n```python\nprint(7 // 2, 7 % 2)      # 3 1\nprint(-7 // 2, -7 % 2)    # -4 1\nprint(divmod(-7, 2))      # (-4, 1)\n```\n`pow(a, b, mod)` performs modular exponentiation efficiently and avoids building the full enormous intermediate result. It is useful in number theory and cryptographic algorithms, though secure cryptography should use vetted libraries.\nCHECK: What is `-7 // 2` in Python? ;; -4 ;; -3 ;; -3.5 ;; Floor division rounds toward negative infinity\n\n## Floating point and numerical stability\nMost decimal fractions, including 0.1, cannot be represented exactly in binary floating point. Small errors are normal; repeated operations can amplify them. Compare tolerances instead of assuming exact equality, and use `decimal.Decimal` for decimal business rules or `fractions.Fraction` when rational exactness is needed.\n```python\nimport math\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)           # False\nprint(math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, .3)) # True\n```\nFor summing many floats, `math.fsum` often reduces accumulated rounding error. Choose the representation based on the domain: floats for scientific approximation, Decimal for decimal accounting rules, integers for cents when possible.\nCHECK: Why can `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` be false? ;; Binary floats approximate most decimal fractions ;; Python randomly changes numbers ;; `+` concatenates numbers ;; The stored binary approximations do not exactly equal the decimal values","Deep Dive: Numbers, Floats & Conversions":"## Representation versus value\nA number's displayed decimal spelling is not necessarily its stored representation. `float.hex()` exposes the exact binary floating-point value as a hexadecimal fraction; `as_integer_ratio()` gives the exact rational represented by a float. These tools are useful when debugging numerical algorithms or reproducing edge cases.\n```python\nx = 0.1\nprint(x.hex())\nprint(x.as_integer_ratio())\n```\nThe ratio is exact for the stored float, even though it is not exactly 1/10. Converting a float to `int` truncates toward zero; `round` uses ties-to-even for halfway cases, so specify the rule your application actually needs.\nCHECK: What does `int(-3.9)` return? ;; -3 ;; -4 ;; -3.9 ;; Converting float to int truncates toward zero\n\n## Choosing conversion boundaries\nParsing should happen at the boundary where untrusted text becomes domain data. `int(text, base)` interprets integer text, while `float(text)` accepts decimal/exponent notation. For financial inputs, parse the original text with `Decimal`; converting a float to Decimal preserves the float's approximation, not the user's original decimal spelling.\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nprice = Decimal(\"19.95\")\nprint(price * 3)\n```\nReject NaN and infinity if a downstream operation assumes finite values (`math.isfinite`). Never assume converting a value makes it valid; validate range and meaning after parsing.\nCHECK: Why is `Decimal(\"0.1\")` preferred to `Decimal(0.1)` for decimal input? ;; It preserves the decimal text exactly ;; It runs on the GPU ;; It converts 0.1 into an integer ;; Constructing Decimal from text avoids importing the float approximation","Text & Number Bases":"## Unicode is not bytes\nA Python `str` is a sequence of Unicode code points; a `bytes` object is a sequence of 8-bit values. Encoding maps text to bytes, and decoding maps bytes back to text. Files and network protocols require an explicit encoding choice because the same bytes can represent different text under different encodings.\n```python\ntext = \"café\"\ndata = text.encode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(data)\nassert data.decode(\"utf-8\") == text\n```\nDo not decode arbitrary binary data as if it were valid text. A `UnicodeDecodeError` often indicates that the wrong encoding was assumed or that the data is not text.\nCHECK: Which operation converts a `str` to UTF-8 bytes? ;; `text.encode(\"utf-8\")` ;; `text.decode(\"utf-8\")` ;; `bytes(text)` without an encoding ;; Encoding translates Unicode text to a chosen byte format\n\n## Number bases and formatting\nInteger literals can use prefixes `0b`, `0o`, and `0x`; formatting functions reveal a number in a different base without changing its value. `int(text, base)` parses text in a selected base. For bit-level reasoning, distinguish a number's mathematical value from its formatted representation.\n```python\nn = int(\"ff\", 16)\nprint(n, bin(n), oct(n), hex(n))\nprint(f\"{n:08b}\")\n```\nFormatting is also useful for diagnostics: binary strings show flags, hexadecimal is compact for bytes, and fixed-width formatting makes fields easier to inspect. Negative integer formatting follows Python's sign-and-magnitude textual conventions, not a fixed-width two's-complement display unless you explicitly mask to a width.\nCHECK: What does `int(\"ff\", 16)` produce? ;; 255 ;; 16 ;; 0xff as text ;; The base argument tells `int` how to interpret the digits","String Methods in Depth":"## Strings are immutable sequences\nString methods return new strings instead of changing the existing object. Methods such as `strip`, `replace`, `casefold`, `split`, and `join` compose into pipelines. `casefold()` is more aggressive than `lower()` for caseless Unicode matching; use it when comparing human language text without case distinctions.\n```python\nraw = \"  Straße, PYTHON  \"\nclean = \" \".join(raw.casefold().replace(\",\", \" \").split())\nprint(clean)\n```\nAvoid repeated concatenation in a large loop when collecting many fragments. Build a list and use `\"\".join(parts)` instead. This is clearer and avoids repeatedly allocating larger intermediate strings.\nCHECK: What is the important property of Python strings? ;; They are immutable ;; Their methods always change them in place ;; They can only contain ASCII ;; Methods such as replace return a new string\n\n## Parsing and safe formatting\n`split()` without an argument splits on runs of whitespace; `split(\",\")` splits on a literal delimiter. `partition()` is convenient when you need exactly the first delimiter and the left/remaining parts. For structured formats such as CSV, JSON, or URLs, use a parser instead of hand-splitting because quoting and escaping rules are easy to get wrong.\n```python\nkey, sep, value = \"mode=fast\".partition(\"=\")\nif not sep:\n    raise ValueError(\"missing =\")\nprint(key, value)\n```\nUse f-strings for readable interpolation, but never treat untrusted text as a format string or Python expression. Presentation formatting and data serialization are different concerns.\nCHECK: Why should CSV data usually be parsed with the `csv` module instead of `split(\",\")`? ;; Quoting and escaped delimiters make naive splitting incorrect ;; `split` is only for integers ;; CSV cannot contain commas ;; A real parser handles the format rules","Logic & Loops":"## Truthiness, short-circuiting, and invariants\nValues such as empty lists, empty strings, zero, and `None` are falsey. `and` and `or` short-circuit and return one of their operands, not necessarily a Boolean. This allows concise fallback expressions, but can be incorrect when a valid value such as `0` should not trigger a fallback.\n```python\ncount = 0\nshown = count if count is not None else 10\nprint(shown)  # 0\n```\nWhen using loops for algorithms, define a loop invariant: a fact that is true before and after each iteration. For binary search, for example, the target—if present—must remain inside the candidate interval. Invariants help reason about correctness rather than just test examples.\nCHECK: If `x = 0`, what does `x or 10` evaluate to? ;; 10 ;; 0 ;; True ;; `or` returns its second operand when the first is falsey\n\n## Iteration and loop control\nA `for` loop consumes an iterator; it does not require a numeric index. Prefer `enumerate(items)` when both index and value are needed, and `zip(a, b, strict=True)` in Python 3.10+ when mismatched lengths should be an error. `break` ends the nearest loop, `continue` skips to its next iteration, and loop `else` runs only if the loop was not terminated by `break`.\n```python\nfor n in range(2, 20):\n    if n == 13:\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"13 was not found\")\n```\nWatch boundary conditions: `range(start, stop)` excludes `stop`. Off-by-one errors often arise from mixing inclusive mathematical intervals with Python's half-open slices and ranges.\nCHECK: When does a loop `else` block execute? ;; When the loop finishes without hitting `break` ;; Every time the loop runs once ;; Only if an exception happens ;; The else belongs to normal loop completion","Collections":"## Mutability and hashability\nLists and dictionaries are mutable; tuples and strings are immutable. Dictionary keys and set elements must be hashable, meaning their hash remains stable while used in the collection. A tuple is hashable only if all its elements are hashable. Never mutate a key in a way that changes equality or hash behavior.\n```python\nlookup = {(\"Seattle\", 2026): \"record\"}\nprint(lookup[(\"Seattle\", 2026)])\n# [1, 2] cannot be a dict key because a list is unhashable.\n```\nA list copy such as `items.copy()` is shallow: nested objects remain shared. Use explicit nested copying or `copy.deepcopy` only when the object graph and semantics justify it.\nCHECK: Why can a tuple containing a list not be used as a dictionary key? ;; Its list element is unhashable ;; Tuples are always mutable ;; Keys must be strings ;; A tuple is hashable only when all its elements are hashable\n\n## Choosing the right collection\nUse a list for ordered sequences and indexed access, a tuple for fixed grouped values, a set for membership and uniqueness, and a dictionary for keyed lookup. `collections.deque` supports efficient operations at both ends; removing the first item repeatedly from a list is expensive because remaining items shift.\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nq = deque([\"job-1\", \"job-2\"])\nq.append(\"job-3\")\nprint(q.popleft())\n```\nA data structure is an algorithmic choice. Consider the operations you perform most often, the expected size, ordering requirements, and whether duplicates matter—not just which syntax feels familiar.\nCHECK: What is usually the best standard-library choice for a FIFO queue with frequent removals at the front? ;; `collections.deque` ;; A list with repeated `pop(0)` ;; A set ;; Deque supports efficient operations at both ends","Collection Methods in Depth":"## Mutation versus returned values\nMethods such as `list.sort`, `list.append`, `dict.update`, and `set.add` mutate their object and return `None`. By contrast, `sorted(iterable)` creates a new list. Confusing these patterns can overwrite a useful variable with `None`.\n```python\nvalues = [3, 1, 2]\nordered = sorted(values)\nvalues.sort()\nprint(ordered, values)\n```\nUse `key=` to sort by a derived property rather than transforming the objects themselves. For multiple criteria, return a tuple from the key function; Python's sort is stable, so equal-key elements retain relative order.\nCHECK: What does `items = items.sort()` typically set `items` to? ;; `None` ;; A sorted list ;; A Boolean ;; `list.sort()` modifies the list in place and returns None\n\n## Views, defaults, and safe updates\nDictionary methods have different contracts. `get` reads without creating a missing key; `setdefault` inserts a default if the key is missing; `update` merges entries. `dict.items()` is a dynamic view, so it reflects subsequent changes to the dictionary and is not a snapshot.\n```python\ncounts = {}\nfor word in [\"red\", \"blue\", \"red\"]:\n    counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)\n```\nIf multiple operations may mutate a dictionary during iteration, iterate over `list(d.items())` to create a snapshot. Prefer `collections.Counter` for frequency counts and `defaultdict(list)` for grouping, when their default behavior is what you want.\nCHECK: What is a key difference between `dict.get(k, 0)` and `dict.setdefault(k, 0)`? ;; `get` does not insert a missing key, but `setdefault` does ;; `get` always mutates the dictionary ;; `setdefault` returns a list only ;; Their missing-key behavior differs","Functions":"## Functions as values and contracts\nFunctions are objects: they can be stored in variables, passed as arguments, returned from other functions, and placed in containers. A good function has a clear contract—inputs, outputs, side effects, and exceptions. Keep pure transformation logic separate from I/O when possible because pure functions are easier to test and reuse.\n```python\ndef normalize(name: str) -> str:\n    return \" \".join(name.split()).casefold()\n\nnames = [\" Ada  LOVELACE \", \"Grace Hopper\"]\nprint(list(map(normalize, names)))\n```\nUse a comprehension when it reads more clearly than `map`; the important idea is that behavior can be passed around independently of a specific call.\nCHECK: In Python, can a function be stored in a variable and called through that variable? ;; Yes, functions are objects ;; No, functions can only be called by their declared names ;; Only async functions can ;; A function value can be passed and called like other objects\n\n## Argument binding and defaults\nPositional arguments bind by order; keyword arguments bind by parameter name. A parameter with a default may be omitted, but default expressions are evaluated once when the `def` statement executes. Mutable defaults are shared across calls, so use `None` as a sentinel and create the object inside the function.\n```python\ndef add_tag(tag, tags=None):\n    if tags is None:\n        tags = []\n    tags.append(tag)\n    return tags\n```\nUse `*args` to collect extra positional arguments and `**kwargs` to collect extra keyword arguments. Do not use them to hide an unclear API; explicit parameters make validation and help easier.\nCHECK: Why is `def f(items=[]): ...` risky? ;; The same list object is reused across calls ;; Python makes a fresh list per call ;; Lists cannot be parameters ;; Default expressions are evaluated once when the function is defined","Deep Dive: Arguments, Closures & Decorators":"## Closures capture bindings\nA closure is a function together with the surrounding lexical environment it references. Nested functions can read enclosing variables; `nonlocal` lets a nested function rebind a variable from the nearest enclosing function scope. Closures are useful for stateful callbacks, factories, and decorators, but hidden mutable state should be documented.\n```python\ndef make_counter():\n    total = 0\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal total\n        total += 1\n        return total\n    return next_value\n```\nA frequent pitfall is late binding in loops: closures usually see the variable's current binding when called, not a frozen copy of its value. Default arguments or a small factory function can capture per-iteration values intentionally.\nCHECK: What does `nonlocal total` allow inside a nested function? ;; Rebinding a variable from an enclosing function scope ;; Creating a global variable ;; Changing an immutable integer in place ;; It changes which scope receives the assignment\n\n## Decorators and descriptor behavior\nA decorator receives a callable and returns a replacement callable or another object. `functools.wraps` preserves useful metadata such as the wrapped function’s name and docstring. A decorator can validate arguments, time execution, or add caching, but it should preserve the wrapped function’s behavior and signature as much as practical.\n```python\nfrom functools import wraps\n\ndef trace(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        print(\"calling\", fn.__name__)\n        return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n```\nDecorator order matters because `@a` above `@b` means `a(b(function))`. For async functions, a normal wrapper that does not await the coroutine can change when errors occur; use an async wrapper for async behavior.\nCHECK: Why use `functools.wraps` in a decorator? ;; To preserve important metadata from the wrapped callable ;; To make the function run in parallel ;; To automatically validate types ;; It helps tooling and debugging identify the original function","Deep Dive: Comprehensions & Generators":"## Laziness and generator state\nA generator function yields a sequence over time and suspends at each `yield`, preserving its local state. This can process large streams without materializing every result. A generator is usually single-use: after it is exhausted, iterating it again produces no values.\n```python\ndef squared_evens(values):\n    for value in values:\n        if value % 2 == 0:\n            yield value * value\n\nprint(sum(squared_evens(range(1_000_000))))\n```\nA list comprehension eagerly creates a list; a generator expression computes each item as requested. Choose eager data when you need repeated access or length, and lazy iteration when the data is large or produced incrementally.\nCHECK: What is a key advantage of a generator over a list for a huge stream? ;; It can yield values without storing the whole result ;; It supports random indexing better ;; It can be iterated forever automatically ;; Generators allow lazy consumption with lower memory use\n\n## Comprehension scope and generator pipelines\nComprehensions express transform/filter steps compactly. Their loop variables do not leak into the surrounding scope in modern Python. Nested comprehensions follow the same left-to-right order as nested `for` loops, so ensure the result is readable rather than merely short.\n```python\npairs = [(x, y) for x in range(3) for y in range(x)]\nprint(pairs)\n```\nPipelines can chain `map`, `filter`, generator expressions, and tools from `itertools`. Beware of consuming an iterator twice: convert it to a list only when you intentionally need to replay it.\nCHECK: What happens to a generator after its values have all been consumed? ;; It is exhausted and normally produces no more values ;; It resets automatically ;; It turns into a list ;; Iterators keep state and are generally one-pass","Errors & Exceptions":"## Exceptions are part of an API\nAn exception signals that an operation could not satisfy its contract. Raise a specific exception such as `ValueError` or `FileNotFoundError` when its meaning is clear, and catch only errors you can actually handle. Broad `except Exception` blocks can hide bugs and make broken state look successful.\n```python\ndef parse_port(text):\n    try:\n        port = int(text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"port must be an integer\") from exc\n    if not 1 <= port <= 65535:\n        raise ValueError(\"port outside valid range\")\n    return port\n```\nException chaining with `raise ... from exc` keeps the underlying cause attached to the higher-level explanation.\nCHECK: Why is `except Exception: pass` usually a bad default? ;; It silently hides unexpected failures ;; It catches only syntax errors ;; It causes every exception to be fatal ;; Broad swallowing destroys diagnostic information\n\n## Cleanup, control flow, and tests\n`finally` executes during normal return and exception unwinding, making it appropriate for cleanup; context managers are usually clearer for files, locks, and transactions. `else` on a `try` runs only when the `try` suite succeeded without an exception. Do not use exceptions for ordinary high-frequency branching when a simple condition is clearer.\n```python\ntry:\n    value = int(raw)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"invalid input\")\nelse:\n    print(\"parsed\", value)\nfinally:\n    print(\"attempt finished\")\n```\nTests should assert both success behavior and failure contracts, including the exception type and where relevant its message.\nCHECK: When does a `try` statement’s `else` suite run? ;; Only if the try suite completes without an exception ;; Whenever the except suite executes ;; Always before finally ;; It separates successful execution from exception handling","Files & Input":"## File handles and resource lifetimes\nUse `with open(...)` so the file is closed even if an exception occurs. Text mode decodes bytes and may translate newlines; binary mode reads and writes bytes unchanged. State an encoding explicitly for text files to avoid machine-dependent defaults.\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\npath = Path(\"notes.txt\")\npath.write_text(\"hello\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\ntext = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\nprint(text)\n```\nChoose modes carefully: `w` truncates an existing file, `a` appends, and `x` creates only if the path does not already exist. For important data, write a temporary file then replace the destination atomically when supported by the filesystem.\nCHECK: What does opening a file in `\"w\"` mode do if it already exists? ;; It truncates the existing contents ;; It always appends ;; It refuses to open ;; Write mode starts a fresh file\n\n## Paths, validation, and streaming\n`pathlib.Path` is easier to compose than manually concatenating path strings. For large files, iterate line by line instead of calling `read()` on the whole file. When accepting filenames from users, constrain them to the intended directory and avoid trusting path segments that might escape it.\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot = Path(\"uploads\").resolve()\ncandidate = (root / user_name).resolve()\nif root not in candidate.parents:\n    raise ValueError(\"path escapes upload directory\")\n```\nThis is a useful defensive check, but secure file handling also needs to consider symlinks, races, permissions, and platform differences. Do not treat a single string check as a complete security boundary.\nCHECK: Why use `Path` and stream large files line-by-line? ;; Clearer path handling and bounded memory use ;; It makes every read asynchronous ;; It eliminates all permission errors ;; Streaming avoids loading the entire file into memory","Context Managers":"## The context manager protocol\nThe `with` statement calls `__enter__` when entering a block and `__exit__` when leaving it, including while an exception unwinds. It provides a structured lifetime for resources such as files, locks, database transactions, and temporary state. If `__exit__` returns a truthy value, it suppresses the active exception—usually something to do only deliberately.\n```python\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef tagged(name):\n    print(\"enter\", name)\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        print(\"exit\", name)\n```\nCode after `yield` behaves like cleanup logic and must be protected with `try/finally` if it must always run.\nCHECK: Which method is called when a `with` block exits? ;; `__exit__` ;; `__enter__` a second time ;; `__del__` ;; The protocol provides deterministic cleanup even on exceptions\n\n## Composing and exception suppression\n`contextlib.ExitStack` manages a dynamic number of context managers, while `contextlib.suppress` ignores only explicitly listed exception types. Async resources use `async with` and `__aenter__`/`__aexit__`. The key design question is ownership: whoever opens or acquires a resource should normally define its cleanup boundary.\n```python\nfrom contextlib import ExitStack\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    files = [stack.enter_context(open(name, encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n             for name in names]\n```\nA context manager should not suppress errors by accident. Preserve exceptions unless recovery is genuine and the resulting state is documented.\nCHECK: What is `ExitStack` particularly useful for? ;; Managing a dynamic number of context managers ;; Making every exception disappear ;; Turning sync functions into coroutines ;; It lets resources be registered gradually and then closed together","Exceptions I: Core Hierarchy":"## Hierarchy and catch ordering\nMost ordinary runtime errors inherit from `Exception`, which itself inherits from `BaseException`. `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, and `GeneratorExit` are direct `BaseException` descendants so a broad `except Exception` does not normally swallow user interrupts or interpreter exit requests. Catch subclasses before broader base classes.\n```python\ntry:\n    int(\"not a number\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"bad numeric text\")\nexcept Exception:\n    print(\"other application error\")\n```\nCatch a group of related errors only when they share one recovery path. If different failures require different action, use distinct handlers.\nCHECK: Why should `except ValueError` usually appear before `except Exception`? ;; The broader handler would also catch ValueError first ;; ValueError is not an exception ;; Python sorts handlers alphabetically ;; A broad handler makes later specialized handlers unreachable\n\n## Designing error boundaries\nTranslate low-level exceptions into domain-level errors at boundaries where the caller needs a more meaningful contract, while preserving the cause. Libraries should avoid catching errors just to re-raise the same exception without adding context. Command-line programs can catch expected top-level failures, display a clean message, and still log diagnostic details for developers.\n```python\nclass ConfigError(Exception):\n    pass\n\ntry:\n    port = parse_port(raw)\nexcept ValueError as exc:\n    raise ConfigError(\"invalid server configuration\") from exc\n```\nDo not use exceptions as data transport for every expected branch; validation results and optional values can be clearer when absence is normal.\nCHECK: Why chain a domain error with `raise NewError(...) from exc`? ;; It preserves the original cause for debugging ;; It deletes the original traceback ;; It turns the error into a warning ;; Exception chaining preserves low-level context","Exceptions II: Files, OS & Imports":"## Differentiate operating-system errors\nFilesystem failures are not all equivalent. `FileNotFoundError`, `PermissionError`, `IsADirectoryError`, and `FileExistsError` indicate different conditions; `OSError` is their broader family. Retrying a missing file is not the same as retrying a temporary network or lock failure, so handle only cases that have a safe recovery strategy.\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntry:\n    data = Path(\"settings.json\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    data = \"{}\"  # only if a missing config is an accepted default\n```\nA fallback is a product decision: if missing configuration would make the app insecure or misleading, fail loudly instead of silently defaulting.\nCHECK: Should every `OSError` be retried automatically? ;; No; the cause and operation must make retry safe ;; Yes, all OS errors are temporary ;; No OS errors can be retried ;; Different subclasses represent different recovery paths\n\n## Import failures and environment diagnosis\n`ModuleNotFoundError` commonly means a package is absent from the active interpreter or a module name is wrong; `ImportError` can mean the module exists but a requested name could not be imported. Virtual environments isolate dependencies, so install packages using the same interpreter that runs the application (`python -m pip ...`).\n```python\nimport sys\nprint(sys.executable)\nprint(sys.path)\n```\nDo not catch every import error and continue as if the dependency were optional. If an optional feature is genuinely optional, catch the specific expected import failure and report how to enable it.\nCHECK: What is a useful first diagnostic when a package imports in one terminal but not another? ;; Check `sys.executable` and the active environment ;; Delete every Python install ;; Catch all exceptions ;; The two commands may be using different interpreters or environments","Exceptions III: Text, Syntax, Control & Warnings":"## Warnings versus failures\nA warning reports a suspicious or deprecated condition while allowing execution to continue; an exception interrupts ordinary flow. Library authors should choose the smallest warning category that communicates a real issue. Users and tests can configure warnings as errors to make deprecations visible early.\n```python\nimport warnings\nwarnings.warn(\"old setting is deprecated\", DeprecationWarning, stacklevel=2)\n```\n`stacklevel` points the warning at the caller rather than inside the helper, which is usually more actionable. Avoid emitting the same warning repeatedly in a hot loop.\nCHECK: What is the primary difference between a warning and an exception? ;; A warning usually allows execution to continue ;; A warning always terminates the process ;; Exceptions are only printed ;; Warnings communicate concerns without stopping normal execution\n\n## Syntax and control exceptions\n`SyntaxError` occurs when source cannot be parsed; `IndentationError` is a related syntax problem. `StopIteration` signals iterator exhaustion and normally should be managed by iteration constructs rather than raised manually in generator bodies. In modern Python, a `StopIteration` escaping a generator is transformed into a `RuntimeError`, preventing accidental silent termination.\n```python\ndef items():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\nfor value in items():\n    print(value)\n```\nUse `warnings.catch_warnings()` or `pytest.warns` in tests to check warnings intentionally. Error handling should preserve the distinction between invalid syntax, exhausted iteration, and a real application failure.\nCHECK: How should ordinary iteration usually handle exhaustion? ;; Let `for` or `next` with a default handle it ;; Raise `StopIteration` manually from every generator ;; Catch `SyntaxError` ;; Iterator protocols already encode exhaustion","Bitwise Operators & Number Systems":"## Masks, shifts, and flags\nBitwise operators treat integers as bit patterns for operations such as masking and flags: `&` AND, `|` OR, `^` XOR, `~` complement, `<<` left shift, and `>>` right shift. Masks let you inspect or change selected bits without disturbing others.\n```python\nREAD = 0b001\nWRITE = 0b010\nEXECUTE = 0b100\npermissions = READ | WRITE\nprint(bool(permissions & WRITE))\npermissions &= ~WRITE\n```\nPython integers have arbitrary precision and negative bit operations behave as if integers had an unbounded two's-complement representation. To model a fixed-width word, explicitly mask with `(1 << width) - 1`.\nCHECK: How do you test whether a flag is set in `flags`? ;; `bool(flags & FLAG)` ;; `flags + FLAG == 1` ;; `flags is FLAG` ;; AND with the bit mask isolates the flag\n\n## Bit tricks need invariants\nFor positive integers, `n & (n - 1)` clears the lowest set bit; this tests powers of two when combined with `n > 0`. `int.bit_count()` counts one-bits. These tricks are compact, but document assumptions about sign and width so readers do not mistake them for universal formulas.\n```python\ndef is_power_of_two(n):\n    return n > 0 and (n & (n - 1)) == 0\n```\nBitwise operations are common in binary protocols, compact flags, hashing, and low-level algorithms. They are not necessarily faster or clearer than arithmetic unless the bit-level property is relevant.\nCHECK: Why does `is_power_of_two` need `n > 0` as well as the bit trick? ;; Zero and negative values must not count as positive powers of two ;; The bit trick only works for floats ;; Python cannot compare integers ;; The precondition excludes zero and negative inputs","Bytes & Raw Data":"## Binary protocols and bytearray\n`bytes` is immutable raw data; `bytearray` is mutable. Indexing either produces an integer from 0 to 255, while slicing produces another bytes-like object. `memoryview` exposes a view over a buffer without copying the whole data, useful when processing large binary payloads.\n```python\npacket = bytearray([0x50, 0x51, 0x01])\npacket[2] = 0x02\nview = memoryview(packet)\nprint(view[0], view[1:])\n```\nFor a binary format, specify byte order explicitly with `int.from_bytes` and `to_bytes` or use `struct`. Do not assume the machine's native byte order matches the protocol.\nCHECK: What does indexing a `bytes` value return? ;; An integer from 0 to 255 ;; A one-character string ;; A new bytes object of any length ;; A byte value is represented by an integer\n\n## Parsing untrusted binary input\nBinary data often has framing: a header tells you the version or payload length, followed by the payload. Validate minimum header length and declared lengths before reading slices. Never trust a length field to be reasonable just because it is an integer.\n```python\ndef read_u16(data, offset):\n    if offset < 0 or offset + 2 > len(data):\n        raise ValueError(\"truncated 16-bit field\")\n    return int.from_bytes(data[offset:offset+2], \"big\")\n```\nUse `struct.unpack_from` for fixed layouts and the `binascii` module for diagnostics. A parser should reject truncated or malformed input deterministically rather than silently interpreting missing bytes as zeros.\nCHECK: What must a parser check before reading a two-byte field at offset `i`? ;; That `i >= 0` and `i + 2 <= len(data)` ;; That the field contains UTF-8 ;; That the bytes are printable ;; Bounds checks stop truncated input from being treated as valid","Objects & Introspection":"## Attribute lookup and introspection\n`getattr`, `setattr`, `hasattr`, `dir`, and `vars` enable inspection, but they do not remove the need for a clear interface. `hasattr(obj, name)` is implemented by attempting attribute access and can therefore interact with properties or custom `__getattr__`. Prefer direct protocol checks or explicit methods when possible.\n```python\nclass Settings:\n    theme = \"dark\"\n\ns = Settings()\nprint(getattr(s, \"theme\", \"light\"))\nprint(vars(s))  # instance attributes, not class attributes\n```\n`vars(obj)` generally reflects the instance dictionary and may fail for slot-only objects. `dir()` is for interactive discovery, not a guarantee that every listed name is a stable public API.\nCHECK: What does `vars(instance)` usually show for an ordinary object? ;; Its instance `__dict__` ;; Every inherited attribute and method ;; Only built-ins ;; It reflects attributes stored directly on the instance\n\n## Representations and contracts\n`repr(obj)` should be a developer-oriented representation; `str(obj)` is intended for display. Implementing `__repr__` makes debugging collections of custom objects much easier. Keep representations unambiguous, avoid exposing secrets, and avoid side effects because debuggers and logs may call them often.\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n@dataclass\nclass Point:\n    x: float\n    y: float\n```\nIntrospection is powerful for frameworks, serializers, and debugging, but dynamic attribute manipulation can make code harder for static analyzers and refactoring tools. Prefer explicit registries and protocols when they express the design adequately.\nCHECK: Why is `__repr__` useful? ;; It gives developers a useful debugging representation ;; It automatically serializes secure data ;; It enforces immutability ;; A good repr helps inspect objects without guessing their state","Deep Dive: Inheritance, Dunders & Dataclasses":"## Method resolution order\nPython uses C3 method resolution order (MRO) to determine which implementation is found when a class has multiple bases. `super()` means “continue cooperative lookup after this class in the MRO,” not simply “call my parent.” Multiple inheritance works best when each participating method uses `super()` consistently and preserves compatible signatures.\n```python\nclass Base:\n    def label(self): return [\"base\"]\nclass Tagged(Base):\n    def label(self): return [\"tag\"] + super().label()\nprint(Tagged().label())\n```\nInspect `SomeClass.__mro__` when lookup behavior is surprising. Avoid multiple inheritance merely to reuse a few lines; composition can be simpler when roles do not form a cooperative hierarchy.\nCHECK: What does `super()` do in cooperative multiple inheritance? ;; Continues method lookup after the current class in the MRO ;; It always calls the first declared parent directly ;; It skips every parent ;; MRO-aware delegation supports cooperative behavior\n\n## Dataclasses and data invariants\n`@dataclass` generates methods such as `__init__` and `__repr__` from annotated fields. Use `field(default_factory=list)` for per-instance mutable defaults. Frozen dataclasses prevent ordinary attribute assignment but do not make every object reachable from the instance deeply immutable. Slots can reduce per-instance memory when the data model supports them.\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n@dataclass\nclass Job:\n    name: str\n    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n```\nDataclasses generate boilerplate, not domain validation. Enforce invariants explicitly, for example in `__post_init__`, and consider whether equality and hashing semantics are appropriate for the objects you model.\nCHECK: Why use `default_factory=list` on a dataclass field? ;; Each instance gets its own list ;; All instances intentionally share one list ;; It makes the list immutable ;; The factory is called separately for each constructed instance","Special Values & Interpreter":"## Sentinels and missing values\n`None` means no value, but sometimes “missing” differs from “present with value None.” A unique sentinel object can represent omission without colliding with valid input. Never use `is` for ordinary value equality; identity comparisons are appropriate for `None` and sentinel objects.\n```python\n_MISSING = object()\ndef get_setting(mapping, key, default=_MISSING):\n    if key in mapping:\n        return mapping[key]\n    if default is _MISSING:\n        raise KeyError(key)\n    return default\n```\nThis avoids ambiguities where `dict.get(key)` cannot tell a missing key from a stored `None`. Sentinels are especially useful in APIs with optional arguments and validation boundaries.\nCHECK: Why use a unique sentinel instead of `None` as a default marker? ;; Because `None` may itself be a valid supplied value ;; Sentinels are always JSON serializable ;; None cannot be compared ;; A unique object distinguishes omission from a legitimate None value\n\n## Interpreter model and environment\nPython source is parsed and compiled to bytecode that the interpreter executes. Implementation details vary across Python implementations and releases, so avoid relying on bytecode layout or object memory addresses for application logic. `sys.version`, `sys.executable`, and `sys.path` are useful when investigating environment-specific behavior.\n```python\nimport sys\nprint(sys.version)\nprint(sys.executable)\nprint(sys.getrecursionlimit())\n```\nThe recursion limit protects the interpreter from runaway Python-level recursion; increasing it is not a substitute for an iterative algorithm when input may be deeply nested. Profile real workloads rather than assuming a language-level operation is slow.\nCHECK: Should ordinary application logic depend on an exact bytecode instruction sequence? ;; No, bytecode details can vary by implementation and release ;; Yes, every Python version has identical bytecode ;; Only when using lists ;; Bytecode is an implementation detail rather than a stable language contract","Code as Data & Namespaces":"## Namespaces and import behavior\nA namespace maps names to objects. Function execution uses local, enclosing, global, and built-in scopes according to Python's name-resolution rules. Imports bind names into the importing module; `import package.module` and `from package.module import name` create different bindings that can matter in tests and monkeypatching.\n```python\nimport math as m\nfrom math import sqrt\nprint(m.sqrt(9), sqrt(16))\n```\nAvoid `from module import *` in application code because it obscures where names came from and can create collisions. Keep modules focused and make dependencies explicit in function arguments when that improves testability.\nCHECK: Why is `from module import *` usually discouraged? ;; It can obscure names and cause collisions ;; It prevents all imports from working ;; It makes names immutable ;; Explicit imports improve readability and debugging\n\n## Dynamic execution is a trust boundary\n`getattr` and registries can dispatch behavior dynamically without compiling strings. `eval` and `exec` can execute arbitrary code and should never be applied to untrusted input. If the goal is to parse data, use a data parser such as `json.loads`; if the goal is choose a named operation, use an allowlisted dictionary of callables.\n```python\noperations = {\"double\": lambda x: x * 2, \"square\": lambda x: x * x}\ndef dispatch(name, value):\n    try:\n        fn = operations[name]\n    except KeyError:\n        raise ValueError(\"unknown operation\")\n    return fn(value)\n```\nDynamic behavior can be elegant, but an explicit set of allowed capabilities is easier to audit than arbitrary source execution.\nCHECK: Why is `eval(user_input)` unsafe? ;; It can execute attacker-controlled expressions ;; It only accepts integers ;; It converts input to JSON ;; Untrusted input must not become executable Python source","Standard Library Tour":"## Build with batteries included\nThe standard library offers modules for common tasks: `pathlib` for paths, `collections` for specialized containers, `itertools` for iterator algebra, `functools` for higher-order utilities, `statistics` and `math` for numerical work, `json` and `csv` for data formats, `logging` for diagnostics, and `argparse` for command-line interfaces. Using a tested standard tool is usually safer than writing a new mini-framework.\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom itertools import islice\nwords = \"red blue red green red\".split()\nprint(Counter(words).most_common(2))\nprint(list(islice(range(1_000_000), 5)))\n```\nKnow the boundaries: `json` is not a general object serializer, `csv` is not a database, and `logging` is not a metrics platform. Match the tool to the job.\nCHECK: Which module provides `Counter`? ;; `collections` ;; `itertools` ;; `json` ;; Counter is a specialized frequency-counting mapping\n\n## Logging, configuration, and CLI design\nUse `logging` rather than scattered `print` statements in larger applications. Configure handlers at the application entry point and let libraries obtain named loggers. `argparse` handles common command-line argument parsing, while `tomllib` reads TOML on Python 3.11+. Use environment variables or config files for deploy-time settings, and never log passwords or access tokens.\n```python\nimport logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\ndef process(count):\n    log.info(\"processing %d records\", count)\n```\nSeparate configuration, core logic, and presentation so the same functionality can be reused from a CLI, a web service, or a test.\nCHECK: Where should a reusable library generally configure global logging handlers? ;; At the application entry point ;; In every library function ;; Inside every exception handler ;; Applications own process-wide logging configuration","match, Walrus & Type Hints":"## Structural pattern matching\n`match`/`case` can destructure shapes and inspect types; it is more than a switch statement. Patterns are tried in order, and an unguarded broad pattern too early can make later cases unreachable. A mapping pattern matches required keys but allows additional keys unless you explicitly check the full shape.\n```python\ndef route(message):\n    match message:\n        case {\"type\": \"ping\", \"id\": ident}:\n            return (\"pong\", ident)\n        case {\"type\": \"data\", \"payload\": payload} if payload:\n            return payload\n        case _:\n            raise ValueError(\"unsupported message\")\n```\nUse pattern matching when the structure of the value drives behavior. For simple scalar comparisons, `if`/`elif` may remain clearer.\nCHECK: What does the final `case _:` pattern represent? ;; A catch-all fallback ;; Only missing dictionary keys ;; An error in pattern syntax ;; The wildcard pattern matches any remaining value\n\n## Type hints and assignment expressions\nType hints improve documentation and enable tools such as mypy and pyright to detect mistakes, but the default interpreter does not enforce them on every call. Use `Protocol` when consumers need a behavior rather than a concrete class. The walrus operator `:=` binds an expression result and can reduce repeated work, but avoid it when it makes control flow harder to scan.\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable\ndef total(values: Iterable[float]) -> float:\n    return sum(values)\n\nif (length := len(\"advanced\")) > 5:\n    print(length)\n```\nPrefer the narrowest useful type and remember that an `Iterable` may be one-shot; a `Sequence` promises indexing and length. Type choices communicate supported operations.\nCHECK: Do Python type annotations normally validate every argument at runtime? ;; No, static type checkers use them unless runtime validation is added ;; Yes, the interpreter rejects every mismatch ;; Only for lists ;; Type hints do not automatically enforce input types","Testing: assert & unittest":"## Test behavior, boundaries, and failures\nA strong test suite checks behavior at the public boundary rather than mirroring every implementation line. Include representative values, edge cases, and invalid inputs. `assert` is useful for internal invariants and small exercises but can be removed by optimized execution; use a test framework for a maintained project.\n```python\ndef clamp(value, low, high):\n    if low > high:\n        raise ValueError(\"low must not exceed high\")\n    return max(low, min(value, high))\n\nassert clamp(12, 0, 10) == 10\n```\nTests should be deterministic, isolated, and fast. Keep fixtures small and avoid dependence on network access, current time, random seeds, or a developer’s filesystem unless that dependency is the thing being tested.\nCHECK: What should a good test suite include beyond a typical successful input? ;; Boundary cases and invalid-input behavior ;; Only large random values ;; Only the current implementation’s private variables ;; Edge cases reveal contract failures missed by happy-path tests\n\n## Regression tests and test doubles\nA regression test captures a bug as a failing example so the same behavior does not return later. Test doubles—stubs, fakes, and mocks—replace external systems when the interaction is part of the contract. Use mocks carefully: a test that asserts every internal call can break during a harmless refactor.\n```python\nimport unittest\nclass ClampTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_upper_bound(self):\n        self.assertEqual(clamp(12, 0, 10), 10)\n    def test_invalid_bounds(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n            clamp(1, 5, 2)\n```\nRun tests in continuous integration and make failures visible. Coverage tells you which code executed, not whether the assertions were meaningful.\nCHECK: Does 100% line coverage prove the program is correct? ;; No, executed lines may still lack meaningful assertions ;; Yes, all bugs are impossible ;; Coverage checks types only ;; Coverage is evidence of execution, not correctness","Async Iteration":"## Async iterators and generators\nAn asynchronous iterator implements `__aiter__` and `__anext__`; `async for` awaits each next item. An async generator uses `async def` with `yield`, allowing it to pause while waiting for data. This is useful when each item arrives through asynchronous I/O, such as a streaming response or message socket.\n```python\nimport asyncio\nasync def ticks(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n        yield i\n\nasync def main():\n    async for item in ticks(3):\n        print(item)\n```\nAsynchronous iteration does not make CPU-bound work magically parallel. Its benefit is allowing a task to yield while waiting so the event loop can run other ready tasks.\nCHECK: What does `async for` do when requesting the next item? ;; Awaits the async iterator’s next operation ;; Starts a new process for each item ;; Makes CPU work parallel automatically ;; Async iteration coordinates values that may require awaiting\n\n## Cancellation and cleanup\nTasks can be cancelled; cancellation is delivered as `asyncio.CancelledError` at an await point. Use `try/finally` to release resources and generally re-raise cancellation after cleanup rather than swallowing it. Async generators that own resources should be closed or exhausted correctly.\n```python\nasync def consume(stream):\n    try:\n        async for item in stream:\n            await handle(item)\n    finally:\n        await stream.aclose()\n```\nUse a timeout for operations that must not wait forever, and define what partial work means when cancellation occurs. Cancellation is a normal control-flow signal in structured asynchronous programs, not necessarily an application error.\nCHECK: Why should cancellation usually be re-raised after cleanup? ;; It preserves the caller’s ability to stop the task ;; It makes cancellation impossible ;; It restarts the event loop ;; Swallowing cancellation can make shutdown hang or violate expectations","Async/Await & asyncio":"## Tasks, awaits, and concurrency\nA coroutine object does not run to completion simply because it was created; it must be awaited or scheduled. `asyncio.gather` runs awaitables concurrently and preserves result order. Use `TaskGroup` on Python 3.11+ for structured concurrency, so child tasks are scoped to a parent block and failures are handled as a group.\n```python\nimport asyncio\nasync def fetch_one(i):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    return i * 2\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(fetch_one(i) for i in range(4)))\n    print(results)\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\nConcurrency helps with latency-bound I/O. For CPU-heavy pure Python loops, use processes, native libraries, or a different computational strategy instead of expecting an event loop to speed them up.\nCHECK: What does `await` do in an async function? ;; Suspends the current coroutine until the awaitable progresses ;; Always starts a new OS thread ;; Forces CPU parallelism ;; Await gives the event loop opportunities to run other tasks\n\n## Backpressure and task ownership\nCreating an unbounded number of tasks can exhaust memory, sockets, or service quotas. Use a bounded queue and a fixed number of workers when the input stream can outpace the consumers. Apply timeouts and retries only with an understanding of idempotency; retrying a non-idempotent payment or write may duplicate effects.\n```python\nqueue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=100)\n# Producers block when the queue is full; consumers drain it.\n```\nA well-designed async system defines task ownership, cancellation behavior, queue capacity, and shutdown order. Those operational details matter as much as the `async def` syntax.\nCHECK: What is bounded queue capacity useful for? ;; It applies backpressure when producers outpace consumers ;; It guarantees no exceptions ;; It makes all operations CPU parallel ;; A maximum size prevents unbounded buffering","Algorithmic Complexity & Amortized Analysis":"## Asymptotic growth\nBig-O describes an upper growth bound as input size grows; it does not tell you the wall-clock time for one particular machine. Common classes are O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), and O(2ⁿ). For nested loops, count how many times the body actually runs; a loop inside a loop is not automatically quadratic if the inner range shrinks geometrically.\n```python\n# O(n): each item is inspected once\ndef contains(items, target):\n    for item in items:\n        if item == target:\n            return True\n    return False\n```\nSeparate worst-case, average-case, and best-case analysis. State the input assumptions, because a hash lookup, tree lookup, and list scan have different complexity and different constant factors.\nCHECK: What does O(n log n) usually tell you? ;; Growth is roughly proportional to n times log n ;; Runtime is exactly n seconds ;; It is always faster than O(n) ;; Big-O describes growth as input size increases\n\n## Amortized cost and space\nAmortized analysis averages the cost across a sequence of operations without relying on probabilistic input. A dynamic array append is usually O(1) amortized even though an occasional resize copies O(n) elements. Total cost over many appends is O(n), so average cost per append is constant.\n```python\nitems = []\nfor i in range(100_000):\n    items.append(i)  # amortized O(1) per append\n```\nSpace complexity includes auxiliary allocations, recursion stacks, and retained references. A solution that is asymptotically faster may need much more memory; for data pipelines, streaming often trades repeated computation for lower memory.\nCHECK: Why is list append usually called amortized O(1)? ;; Expensive resizes are spread across many cheap appends ;; Every append copies the whole list ;; Its cost is always exactly identical ;; The total resizing work across n appends is linear","Recursion, Memoization & Stack Limits":"## Prove the base case and progress\nA recursive function needs a base case and a step that moves toward it. A useful proof has two parts: base cases are correct, and assuming smaller calls are correct, the current call produces the correct result. If a call does not reduce the problem, it may recurse forever until `RecursionError`.\n```python\ndef sum_to(n):\n    if n <= 0:\n        return 0\n    return n + sum_to(n - 1)\n```\nBefore writing recursion, define what each call promises to return. That contract makes the recurrence easier to reason about than tracing arbitrary stack frames.\nCHECK: What two ingredients does a well-founded recursive function need? ;; A correct base case and progress toward it ;; A global variable and a while loop ;; A decorator and a class ;; Each recursive path must eventually reach a base case\n\n## Memoization and stack depth\nMemoization saves results for repeated inputs. For Fibonacci-like recurrences, caching reduces exponential repeated work to O(n) distinct states, but recursive call depth remains O(n). An iterative dynamic program can use O(1) extra space when only a fixed number of previous states are needed.\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef fib(n):\n    if n < 2: return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n```\nDo not reflexively increase `sys.setrecursionlimit`: deeply recursive algorithms can exhaust the C stack or crash the process. If input depth is untrusted, an explicit stack or iterative algorithm is often safer.\nCHECK: What does memoization improve in a recurrence with repeated subproblems? ;; It avoids recomputing the same state ;; It eliminates every stack frame ;; It makes recursion tail-call optimized ;; Cached states can reduce repeated work while recursion depth may still remain","Dynamic Programming & State Design":"## Define a state before a loop\nDynamic programming (DP) works when a problem can be decomposed into overlapping subproblems and an optimal answer can be built from smaller answers. State what `dp[i]` means in one sentence before coding. Then define the base cases and transition formula; code is just a schedule for evaluating that recurrence.\n```python\n# dp[a] = fewest coins needed to make amount a\n# dp[0] = 0; dp[a] = min(dp[a - coin] + 1)\n```\nA state that omits necessary information leads to incorrect reuse. For example, bounded coin use may require tracking both amount and which item index is allowed next.\nCHECK: What should you define first when designing DP? ;; The exact meaning of each state ;; The variable names in the final loop ;; A random cache size ;; A state definition determines what each stored value guarantees\n\n## Tabulation, memory, and reconstruction\nTop-down DP uses recursion and memoization; bottom-up DP fills a table in dependency order. Space can sometimes be compressed when a new row depends only on a small number of previous rows. But compression must preserve update order: iterating forward versus backward can change whether an item is reused in a knapsack problem.\n```python\n# For 0/1 knapsack, walk capacity downward\nfor weight, value in items:\n    for cap in range(limit, weight - 1, -1):\n        dp[cap] = max(dp[cap], dp[cap-weight] + value)\n```\nIf you need an actual solution rather than its score, keep parent decisions or reconstruct by checking which transition produced the stored value.\nCHECK: Why does 0/1 knapsack usually iterate capacity downward for each item? ;; To avoid using the same item multiple times in the same pass ;; To sort the weights ;; To avoid storing a DP array ;; Downward traversal prevents current-item updates from being reused","Data Structures: Stacks, Queues & Heaps":"## Stack versus queue versus heap\nA stack is last-in, first-out; a queue is first-in, first-out; a priority queue returns the item with the best priority. A Python list is a good stack with `append`/`pop`; `collections.deque` is a good queue; `heapq` implements a min-heap. A heap is not fully sorted—only its root is guaranteed to be the smallest item.\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nstack = [1, 2]; stack.append(3); print(stack.pop())\nqueue = deque([1, 2]); queue.append(3); print(queue.popleft())\n```\nState the invariant clearly: a stack remembers nesting, a queue preserves arrival order, and a heap prioritizes the next item.\nCHECK: Which data structure is the natural choice for retrieving the smallest-priority item repeatedly? ;; A heap / priority queue ;; A set with no ordering ;; A list using `pop(0)` every time ;; `heapq` maintains the smallest item at its root\n\n## Monotonic stacks and lazy deletion\nA monotonic stack keeps values increasing or decreasing to answer next-greater-element and span-style questions in linear time. Each item is pushed and popped at most once. For heaps with changing priorities, Python's `heapq` does not offer a decrease-key operation; common strategies include pushing a new entry with a version and ignoring stale entries when popped.\n```python\ndef next_greater(values):\n    out = [-1] * len(values)\n    stack = []\n    for i, value in enumerate(values):\n        while stack and values[stack[-1]] < value:\n            out[stack.pop()] = value\n        stack.append(i)\n    return out\n```\nThe proof often comes from accounting: once an index is popped, it never returns, so nested `while` loops can still total O(n) work.\nCHECK: Why can a monotonic-stack algorithm with a nested `while` loop still be O(n)? ;; Each index is pushed and popped at most once ;; The inner loop always runs exactly once ;; Python optimizes all loops ;; Amortized counting bounds total stack operations","Graph Algorithms: BFS, DFS & Dijkstra":"## Represent a graph\nA graph contains vertices and edges; edges may be directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. An adjacency list maps each vertex to its neighbors and stores O(V + E) data. An adjacency matrix uses O(V²) space but makes checking whether a particular edge exists constant-time.\n```python\ngraph = {\"A\": [\"B\", \"C\"], \"B\": [\"A\"], \"C\": [\"A\"]}\n```\nDecide whether missing vertices should behave as having no neighbors or trigger an error. That is a data-model contract, not merely an implementation detail.\nCHECK: When is an adjacency list especially useful? ;; Sparse graphs where storing every possible edge would waste space ;; Every possible pair has an edge ;; You need O(1) indexing by matrix coordinate ;; It stores only actual neighbors for a sparse graph\n\n## BFS, DFS, and weighted shortest paths\nBreadth-first search (BFS) explores by distance layers and finds shortest paths in an unweighted graph. Depth-first search (DFS) explores deeply and is useful for reachability, cycle detection, and topological algorithms. Mark nodes visited when enqueuing or pushing so cycles do not repeatedly add them.\n```python\n# BFS: queue contains the current frontier\n# Dijkstra: priority queue contains tentative (distance, vertex) pairs\n```\nDijkstra requires non-negative edge weights. With negative edges, use another algorithm such as Bellman–Ford when its assumptions fit. When paths matter, store a predecessor map and reconstruct the route by walking backward from the destination.\nCHECK: Why does ordinary BFS find shortest paths in an unweighted graph? ;; It visits nodes in increasing edge-distance layers ;; It always explores the alphabetically first node ;; It sorts all edges by weight ;; FIFO ordering ensures every shallower frontier is processed first","Greedy Algorithms & Correctness":"## Greedy choice needs proof\nA greedy algorithm commits to a local choice without reconsidering it. Sorting intervals by end time maximizes the number of non-overlapping intervals because an interval that finishes earliest leaves at least as much room for future choices. A correctness argument is needed; a plausible intuition alone is not proof.\n```python\nintervals = [(1, 4), (3, 5), (4, 7)]\nby_finish = sorted(intervals, key=lambda pair: pair[1])\n```\nExchange arguments show that an optimal solution can be transformed to include the greedy choice without making the solution worse. This is a common proof technique for scheduling and spanning-tree algorithms.\nCHECK: What does an exchange argument establish? ;; An optimal solution can be changed to include the greedy choice without worsening it ;; Every greedy algorithm is correct ;; The code uses less memory ;; It connects a local choice to an optimal solution\n\n## Recognize greedy counterexamples\nCoin change is not greedy-correct for every currency system. With coins 1, 3, and 4, choosing the largest coin first for amount 6 gives 4 + 1 + 1, while 3 + 3 uses fewer coins. This counterexample shows why a general claim needs evidence or proof.\n```python\ncoins = [1, 3, 4]\n# For amount 6: greedy gives [4, 1, 1], optimum is [3, 3].\n```\nA good algorithm workflow is to test small counterexamples, identify the required property, and then prove the claim. For optimization problems, compare candidate greedy choices against DP on small generated cases.\nCHECK: What does the coin set 1, 3, 4 demonstrate for amount 6? ;; Largest-coin-first is not always optimal ;; Greedy is always better than DP ;; Dynamic programming cannot solve change ;; A counterexample disproves the universal greedy claim","Sorting, Searching & Hash Table Internals":"## Stable sorting and binary search\nPython's built-in sort is stable: values with equal keys retain their input order. A key function is evaluated to derive ordering; it is usually cleaner than repeatedly computing attributes inside comparisons. Binary search needs sorted data and a precise interval invariant. `bisect_left` finds the first insertion point that keeps the sequence sorted.\n```python\nfrom bisect import bisect_left\nvalues = [1, 2, 2, 2, 5]\ni = bisect_left(values, 2)\nprint(i)  # 1\n```\nThe insertion point is not always a found index: check bounds and equality. Binary search reduces the candidate range geometrically, so it takes O(log n) comparisons on a random-access sorted sequence.\nCHECK: What does `bisect_left` return when the target is absent? ;; The index where it can be inserted while preserving order ;; Always -1 ;; The nearest value itself ;; It returns an insertion position rather than a membership result\n\n## Hashing, collisions, and contracts\nA dictionary maps hashable keys to values. Hash tables use a hash to narrow the search, then equality checks to resolve collisions. Average lookup and insertion are O(1), but adversarial collisions can worsen performance. The key contract is that equal objects must have equal hashes and a key's hash should not change while stored.\n```python\ncache = {(\"model\", 3): \"ready\"}\nprint(cache.get((\"model\", 3)))\n```\nDo not derive security guarantees from a single hash function detail. Python randomizes hashes for some types, but this is not a reason to accept unbounded user-controlled workloads without input limits.\nCHECK: What must be true for two keys that compare equal? ;; They must have the same hash ;; They must have different hashes ;; Their repr strings must match ;; Hash tables depend on compatible equality and hash behavior","Python Protocols & Structural Typing":"## Nominal and structural typing\nNominal typing asks whether a value belongs to a declared class or subclass. Structural typing asks whether it provides the required operations. Python duck typing has long used the structural idea at runtime; `typing.Protocol` lets static type checkers express those expectations explicitly.\n```python\nfrom typing import Protocol\nclass HasLength(Protocol):\n    def __len__(self) -> int: ...\ndef describe(value: HasLength) -> str:\n    return f\"length={len(value)}\"\n```\nA protocol describes the minimum behavior a consumer needs. It reduces coupling: a class can satisfy the interface without importing or inheriting from the module that defines it.\nCHECK: What is the main purpose of `Protocol`? ;; Describe required behavior without requiring explicit inheritance ;; Force every class to inherit from one base class ;; Validate all values at runtime ;; Structural typing focuses on available operations\n\n## Generics, variance, and API boundaries\nGeneric types preserve relationships between inputs and outputs, such as a function mapping a list of T to a list of T. Variance matters when substituting container types: a read-only producer can safely be more specific in some contexts, while mutable containers are generally invariant because they are both read and written.\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable\ndef first(items: Iterable[str]) -> str | None:\n    return next(iter(items), None)\n```\nUse `Iterable[T]` if you only need iteration, `Sequence[T]` if you need length and indexing, and `Mapping[K, V]` if you only read by key. Narrow requirements make your API more reusable.\nCHECK: When should a parameter be typed as `Iterable[T]` instead of `list[T]`? ;; When the function only needs to iterate ;; Whenever it will mutate list elements ;; When it needs random indexing guaranteed ;; A smaller contract admits generators, tuples, and lists","Descriptors & Attribute Lookup":"## The descriptor protocol\nAn object defining `__get__`, `__set__`, or `__delete__` can control attribute access when stored on a class. A descriptor with `__set__` or `__delete__` is a data descriptor; a descriptor with only `__get__` is non-data. Data descriptors generally take precedence over the instance dictionary, which is why a `property` can validate assignment.\n```python\nclass Celsius:\n    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):\n        return obj.__dict__.get(\"_c\", 0)\n    def __set__(self, obj, value):\n        if value < -273.15: raise ValueError(\"below absolute zero\")\n        obj.__dict__[\"_c\"] = value\n```\nDescriptors power properties, methods, ORM fields, and many frameworks. You can often use `property` rather than writing a descriptor directly; custom descriptors shine when the behavior is reused across many attributes.\nCHECK: Which kind of descriptor generally wins over an instance dictionary entry? ;; A data descriptor ;; A local variable ;; Any plain integer ;; Data descriptors define assignment or deletion behavior\n\n## Attribute lookup details\nWhen `obj.name` is evaluated, Python applies type-level lookup rules, descriptor methods, and the instance dictionary in a defined order. Functions stored on a class are non-data descriptors; access through an instance creates a bound method with the instance supplied as `self`. `__set_name__` lets a descriptor learn the attribute name during class creation.\n```python\nclass Field:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.name = name\n```\nThis power has tradeoffs: descriptors are shared class objects, so per-instance values belong on the instance. Do not store one instance's mutable runtime state directly on the descriptor unless sharing is intentional.\nCHECK: Why should ordinary per-instance descriptor data usually live in `obj.__dict__` or equivalent storage? ;; The descriptor object is shared by all instances of the class ;; Every instance has a unique class descriptor ;; Descriptors cannot read instance attributes ;; Class-level descriptors are shared across instances","Metaclasses & Class Creation":"## Classes are created too\nMost classes are instances of `type`. A metaclass customizes class creation, often by controlling the namespace or validating attributes as the class is defined. For many ordinary use cases, a class decorator or `__init_subclass__` is simpler and easier to explain than a custom metaclass.\n```python\nclass RegistryBase:\n    registry = {}\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        key = getattr(cls, \"kind\", None)\n        if key: RegistryBase.registry[key] = cls\n```\nKeep registration behavior explicit and deterministic. If imports trigger plugin discovery, document that side effect and avoid surprising network or filesystem work at import time.\nCHECK: What simpler hook can often validate or register subclasses without a metaclass? ;; `__init_subclass__` ;; `__del__` ;; `__iter__` ;; It runs when a subclass is defined\n\n## Metaclass tradeoffs\nA metaclass can customize `__prepare__`, class namespace construction, and the resulting class object, but it adds a new layer to inheritance and debugging. Multiple metaclasses can conflict, and frameworks using one can be difficult to compose. Prefer a metaclass only when the behavior truly belongs to class creation and cannot be expressed cleanly otherwise.\n```python\nclass UpperAttrs(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        cleaned = {k.upper() if not k.startswith(\"__\") else k: v\n                   for k, v in namespace.items()}\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, cleaned)\n```\nIn production code, avoiding such namespace transformations is often wise because they obscure attribute names and frustrate tools. Powerful does not mean maintainable.\nCHECK: What is a sound reason to avoid a custom metaclass? ;; A simpler class hook solves the requirement clearly ;; Python cannot inherit from metaclasses ;; Metaclasses cannot inspect namespaces ;; Complex class creation machinery increases coupling and debugging cost","Memory, Garbage Collection, Copies & Weakrefs":"## References, copies, and cycles\nNames hold references to objects. A shallow copy duplicates the outer container but reuses nested references; a deep copy recursively copies many contained objects, subject to custom copy behavior and memoization for cycles. Neither is automatically the right semantic choice—sometimes a deliberate, targeted copy is better.\n```python\nimport copy\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\na[0].append(3)\n```\nCPython primarily uses reference counting plus a cyclic garbage collector for unreachable reference cycles. Do not rely on immediate finalization for external resources; use context managers to close files, sockets, and locks.\nCHECK: What does a shallow copy of a nested list preserve? ;; References to the same nested objects ;; Independent copies of every nested object ;; No references at all ;; Only the outer container is duplicated\n\n## Weak references and memory diagnosis\nA weak reference points to an object without keeping it alive solely because the weak reference exists. Weak-key dictionaries and weak-value dictionaries are useful for caches tied to object lifetime, but the referenced type must support weak references. Weakrefs do not fix a memory leak caused by a still-live strong reference.\n```python\nimport tracemalloc\ntracemalloc.start()\n# run a representative workload here\ncurrent, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\nprint(current, peak)\n```\nMeasure before optimizing. `tracemalloc` can identify allocations attributed to Python code; process RSS includes other native allocations. Releasing a reference makes an object eligible for collection, but memory allocators may keep released arenas for reuse rather than returning them to the OS immediately.\nCHECK: Does creating a weak reference usually keep the target object alive? ;; No, a weak reference does not itself keep the target alive ;; Yes, it is equivalent to a strong reference ;; Only if the object is a list ;; Weak references are meant to observe lifetime without owning it","Threads, Processes & the GIL":"## I/O concurrency versus CPU parallelism\nThreads share one process and its memory. They are useful for overlapping blocking I/O, but the CPython Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) traditionally prevents multiple threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously in a single interpreter. Native extensions may release the GIL, and interpreter builds or future versions can differ, so benchmark the actual workload.\n```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:\n    results = list(pool.map(fetch_one, urls))\n```\nFor CPU-bound pure Python work, multiple processes can use multiple cores at the cost of startup, serialization, and inter-process communication. Async I/O is another option when the libraries support it.\nCHECK: What is a common use for threads in standard CPython? ;; Overlapping blocking I/O operations ;; Automatically speeding up pure Python CPU loops ;; Eliminating the need for locks ;; Threads are often useful while tasks wait on I/O\n\n## Race conditions and process boundaries\nA race condition occurs when correctness depends on the timing of concurrent operations. The GIL does not make multi-step application invariants atomic; protect shared state with locks, queues, or message passing. Processes have separate memory by default, so sharing results requires explicit communication or shared-memory facilities.\n```python\nfrom threading import Lock\nlock = Lock()\ncount = 0\ndef increment():\n    global count\n    with lock:\n        count += 1\n```\nPrefer minimizing shared mutable state over adding locks everywhere. For processes on cross-platform applications, keep the entry point guarded with `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` and make submitted functions and data serializable where required.\nCHECK: Does the GIL make every multi-step update thread-safe? ;; No, application invariants still need synchronization ;; Yes, all sequences are atomic ;; Only when using a list ;; The GIL is not a substitute for locks or message passing","Asyncio: Cancellation & Backpressure":"## Structured concurrency and cancellation\nA task should have a clear owner and lifetime. `asyncio.TaskGroup` (Python 3.11+) scopes child tasks to a block and waits for them before leaving; failures propagate in a structured way. Cancellation is delivered at an await point, so cleanup belongs in `try/finally` and cancellation should generally not be swallowed.\n```python\nasync def serve():\n    try:\n        await handle_requests()\n    finally:\n        await close_connections()\n```\nUse `asyncio.timeout()` where available or `wait_for` for bounded waits. Retries should be limited and used only when repeating the action is safe; cancelled requests may already have completed remotely.\nCHECK: Why should async cleanup use `finally`? ;; Cancellation and exceptions can interrupt the normal path ;; Async functions never raise exceptions ;; Finally blocks make code parallel ;; It ensures cleanup executes during unwinding\n\n## Backpressure, queues, and semaphores\nBackpressure prevents producers from creating work faster than consumers can handle it. `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` blocks producers when the queue is full, while a semaphore limits concurrent access to a resource such as a rate-limited API. Task creation should be bounded for large streams.\n```python\nqueue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=50)\nlimit = asyncio.Semaphore(8)\nasync def limited_call(item):\n    async with limit:\n        return await call_service(item)\n```\nA complete design specifies queue size, error reporting, retry budgets, task cancellation, and shutdown signals. Without these, a system can appear responsive in a demo but fail under load.\nCHECK: What does a bounded async queue provide? ;; Backpressure when work arrives faster than it can be consumed ;; Automatic retry of every failure ;; Unlimited buffering ;; Producers wait when the configured queue capacity is reached","Test Engineering & Property-Based Testing":"## Examples versus properties\nExample tests verify specific cases. Property-based tests verify general laws over many generated inputs: a sort is ordered and preserves the multiset, a serializer round-trips valid data, and a normalization function is idempotent. Hypothesis can generate edge cases and shrink failures to smaller counterexamples.\n```python\n# Property idea: normalize(normalize(x)) == normalize(x)\n# Property idea: decode(encode(value)) == value\n```\nA property must be true for every input in its stated domain. State preconditions clearly; a bad property can reject valid implementations or accidentally test only your assumptions.\nCHECK: Which is a useful property of an idempotent normalizer? ;; Applying it twice has the same result as applying it once ;; It always returns a list ;; It never raises on every possible object ;; Idempotence is a general behavioral law\n\n## Isolation, fixtures, and regression suites\nA test should control dependencies such as time, randomness, filesystem paths, network access, and environment variables. Fixtures arrange repeatable setup; parametrization applies one contract to many cases. Mocks are useful when interaction itself matters, but overly detailed call expectations create brittle tests.\n```python\n# Examples: empty input, one item, duplicates, invalid values,\n# extremely large input, and the failure mode promised by the API.\n```\nWhen a production bug is found, write a regression test before or alongside the fix. Keep the failing input small, deterministic, and specific enough that the test documents the defect.\nCHECK: Does high code coverage guarantee the assertions are meaningful? ;; No, coverage only shows that code ran ;; Yes, every bug must be covered ;; Coverage automatically proves correctness ;; Execution coverage is not the same as behavioral verification","Profiling & Performance Engineering":"## Profile before optimizing\nPerformance work begins with a reproducible baseline and a realistic workload. `timeit` is good for small snippets; `cProfile` can show which functions account for time across a program; `tracemalloc` tracks many Python allocations. Measure several runs and focus on robust differences, not a single tiny timing.\n```python\nimport cProfile\ncProfile.run(\"sum(i*i for i in range(100_000))\")\n```\nDistinguish algorithmic cost from constant factors, I/O wait, network latency, and startup overhead. A microbenchmark can be misleading when its data shape or cache behavior differs from production.\nCHECK: Which tool is appropriate for a first function-level CPU profile? ;; `cProfile` ;; `copy.deepcopy` ;; `json.dumps` ;; Profiling aggregates execution costs by function\n\n## Complexity and memory tradeoffs\nOptimize the largest bottleneck first, then profile again. Replacing an O(n²) algorithm with O(n log n) often matters more than micro-optimizing a line. Memory optimizations can introduce recomputation, latency, or complexity; choose based on measured requirements.\n```python\n# A generator may reduce peak memory for one-pass work.\ntotal = sum(value * value for value in range(1_000_000))\n```\nPreserve correctness with regression tests and compare outputs before and after. For benchmarks, avoid logging in the timed region, control random inputs, warm up where relevant, and state the interpreter and machine used.\nCHECK: What is a good optimization loop? ;; Measure, change one bottleneck, then measure again ;; Rewrite everything at once ;; Guess which line is slow ;; Measurements prevent spending effort on non-dominant code","Packaging, Imports & Dependency Isolation":"## Packages and environments\nA virtual environment isolates installed packages for one project. Activate it for convenience, or invoke its Python directly. Use `python -m pip` so pip is tied to the interpreter you intend to run. Avoid installing project dependencies into a system Python used by unrelated applications.\n```text\nproject/\n  pyproject.toml\n  src/my_package/__init__.py\n  tests/test_core.py\n```\nA package should have a deliberate public API. Keep tests, development tooling, and runtime dependencies distinguishable, and use a lock file or constraints strategy when exact reproducibility matters.\nCHECK: Why use `python -m pip` instead of a bare `pip` command? ;; It ties package installation to the selected Python interpreter ;; It installs packages into every environment ;; It creates a virtual environment automatically ;; It reduces interpreter mismatch problems\n\n## Imports and build metadata\nPython imports depend on `sys.path`, package layout, and installed metadata. Running a file directly from an unexpected directory can cause a different module with the same name to be imported. An installable package and a `pyproject.toml` make project behavior more consistent than depending on the current working directory.\n```toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=68\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n```\nFor deployment, pin or constrain critical dependencies, test in a clean environment, and build an artifact such as a wheel. Avoid relying on undeclared packages that happen to be installed on the developer laptop.\nCHECK: What should a clean-environment install test reveal? ;; Whether all required dependencies and package metadata are declared ;; Whether the developer has many global packages ;; Whether imports can always use the current directory ;; Clean installs expose undeclared dependencies","Secure Python: Injection & Serialization":"## Data is not executable code\nNever pass untrusted text to `eval`, `exec`, shell commands, or unsafe deserializers. JSON provides a data format for primitive structures; `pickle` can execute code during loading and must never be used on untrusted input. Validate the parsed shape and allowed values, not merely whether parsing succeeded.\n```python\nimport json\nobj = json.loads(payload)\nif not isinstance(obj, dict) or obj.get(\"mode\") not in {\"safe\", \"fast\"}:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid config\")\n```\nAvoid building SQL statements by concatenating user values. Use parameterized queries so the database treats values as data rather than SQL syntax.\nCHECK: Why must untrusted data not be loaded with `pickle.loads`? ;; Pickle deserialization can execute attacker-controlled behavior ;; Pickle only supports numbers ;; Pickle always loses data ;; The serialized object can trigger code during loading\n\n## Principle of least privilege\nValidate at trust boundaries, limit path and resource access, protect secrets from logs, and grant only the permissions a component needs. Escaping is context-specific: HTML, SQL, URLs, shell arguments, and JSON each have different rules. There is no universal function that safely quotes every context.\n```python\nimport subprocess\nsubprocess.run([\"tool\", \"--name\", user_value], check=True)\n```\nPassing an argument list avoids shell metacharacter interpretation, but the invoked program may still interpret the value as an option or filename. Use allowlists, option terminators where supported, size limits, and robust validation for the specific command.\nCHECK: Does escaping for HTML make a string safe for SQL? ;; No, escaping is context-specific ;; Yes, all contexts use the same rules ;; Only if the string is short ;; Every output context has its own syntax and defenses","HTTP Clients, Retries & Rate Limits":"## Timeouts and status classification\nEvery network request should have explicit connect/read or overall timeouts. A server can accept a connection but never return a response, so an unbounded wait can tie up resources indefinitely. Separate transport failures from HTTP status codes: a completed response with 404 is different from a connection reset.\n```python\n# With a library such as requests:\n# response = requests.get(url, timeout=(3.05, 10))\n# response.raise_for_status()\n```\nCheck the API contract before deciding which statuses are retryable. Most client errors are not fixed by repeating the same request, while transient server or throttling responses may be retryable under limits.\nCHECK: Why should an HTTP client set timeouts? ;; To prevent requests from waiting indefinitely ;; To guarantee the server responds successfully ;; To bypass rate limits ;; Timeouts bound resource use during stalled operations\n\n## Backoff, idempotency, and rate limits\nRetries amplify traffic during an outage. Use a maximum attempt count, exponential backoff, jitter, and any server-provided `Retry-After` guidance. Retry only when the operation is safe to repeat or an idempotency key prevents duplicate effects. A timeout after a POST does not prove the server did not process it.\n```python\ndef backoff(attempt, base=0.25, cap=10.0):\n    return min(cap, base * (2 ** attempt))\n```\nA rate limiter shapes outgoing traffic; a concurrency limit controls how many calls are in flight. These solve different problems. Record attempt counts and final failures, but avoid logging authorization headers or sensitive request bodies.\nCHECK: Why is retrying a timed-out payment POST dangerous without idempotency? ;; The server may have processed the first request already ;; POST requests cannot contain data ;; Every timeout means the server crashed ;; A repeated non-idempotent request can duplicate the side effect","SQL Transactions & Data Pipelines":"## Queries and transactions\nUse parameterized queries, never concatenate user-provided values into SQL. A transaction groups updates so either the logical operation succeeds or it can be rolled back. ACID describes atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability; real guarantees depend on the database and configuration.\n```python\ncon.execute(\"INSERT INTO item(name) VALUES (?)\", (name,))\n```\nKeep transactions short and ensure failures cause rollback when needed. Do not hold a database transaction open while waiting on a slow HTTP request unless the design specifically requires it.\nCHECK: What does a transaction provide for a group of related writes? ;; A unit that can commit or roll back together ;; Automatic encryption of every field ;; A guarantee that all queries are fast ;; Transactions protect consistency across related changes\n\n## ETL, idempotency, and validation\nETL means extract, transform, load. A good ingestion pipeline validates inputs, normalizes schema, deduplicates records, batches writes, and records failures so the operation can resume. Idempotent stages can be rerun without duplicating effects, often using stable keys or upserts.\n```python\n# Parse -> validate -> normalize -> deduplicate -> write in a transaction\n```\nUse explicit schema migration strategy and treat malformed records deliberately: reject, quarantine, or fix them according to a policy. Silent coercion can corrupt data while making the pipeline appear successful.\nCHECK: Why is idempotency useful in a data pipeline? ;; Rerunning a stage does not duplicate its intended effect ;; It makes malformed input valid ;; It removes the need for transactions ;; Idempotency supports safe retries and recovery","Numerical Computing with NumPy":"## Arrays, shapes, and dtypes\nA NumPy `ndarray` stores a typed, usually homogeneous block of numeric data with a shape and strides. Unlike a Python list of separate objects, array elements use a fixed dtype, making vectorized operations efficient. Shape describes the dimensions; dtype describes the element representation; strides describe how indices map to memory.\n```numpy\nimport numpy as np\nx = np.arange(12, dtype=np.float32).reshape(3, 4)\nprint(x.shape, x.dtype, x.ndim)\n```\nKeep dimensions explicit. A vector of shape `(4,)` is not identical to a column matrix `(4, 1)`. Many numerical bugs are shape bugs that can be caught with assertions close to the data boundary.\nCHECK: What does `x.shape` describe for a NumPy array? ;; The length of each dimension ;; The data type only ;; The array maximum ;; Shape records the array dimensions\n\n## Broadcasting and vectorization\nBroadcasting allows elementwise operations across compatible shapes by comparing dimensions from the right: dimensions must match or one of them must be 1. For example `(3, 1)` and `(1, 4)` broadcast to `(3, 4)`. Broadcasting does not generally copy the small input into a large physical array; it defines how the operation views it.\n```numpy\nx = np.arange(3).reshape(3, 1)\ny = np.arange(4).reshape(1, 4)\nprint((x + y).shape)  # (3, 4)\n```\nVectorization moves loops into optimized array operations, but it can create large intermediates. Use axis reductions intentionally and profile memory for large arrays. Check dtype promotion when mixing integers, floats, and lower-precision values.\nCHECK: What shape results from broadcasting `(3, 1)` with `(1, 4)`? ;; `(3, 4)` ;; `(3, 1, 4)` ;; `(1, 1)` ;; Each of the mismatched dimensions expands to their compatible maximum","PyTorch 1: Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting":"## Tensor mental model\nA PyTorch tensor is a multidimensional typed array with shape, dtype, device, and layout. Most model inputs are tensors with batch and feature dimensions; image batches are often `(N, C, H, W)`. Shape errors usually mean a dimension was omitted, swapped, or interpreted differently than intended.\n```pytorch\nimport torch\nx = torch.randn(32, 3, 224, 224)  # N, C, H, W\nprint(x.shape, x.dtype, x.device)\n```\nCreate tensors with an explicit dtype when precision matters. Integer labels for class-index losses are commonly `torch.long`; neural network weights usually use floating dtypes. Avoid creating a tensor from nested Python lists in a hot loop when data can be batched efficiently.\nCHECK: What do the dimensions in `(32, 3, 224, 224)` commonly mean for image input? ;; Batch, channels, height, width ;; Height, width, batch, labels ;; One 32-channel image ;; The common convention is NCHW image batches\n\n## Broadcasting and device movement\nBroadcasting compares dimensions from the right. Matching dimensions or a size of 1 are compatible; incompatible dimensions raise a runtime error. A `(batch, features)` tensor and a `(features,)` bias usually combine as intended. Be deliberate about adding singleton dimensions with `unsqueeze` and avoid accidental broadcasting that produces a much larger tensor than expected.\n```pytorch\nbias = torch.zeros(64)\nactivations = torch.randn(16, 64)\nresult = activations + bias  # (16, 64)\n```\nTensors and models must be on compatible devices. Move both model and data to the same selected device, and do not assume CUDA exists. `.view()` generally requires compatible strides; `.reshape()` may copy when needed. Check shapes after transformations while learning.\nCHECK: If shapes are `(16, 64)` and `(64,)`, what is the result shape after addition? ;; `(16, 64)` ;; `(16, 128)` ;; `(64, 16)` ;; The one-dimensional bias broadcasts across the batch dimension","PyTorch 2: Autograd & Gradient Debugging":"## The computation graph\nAutograd records tensor operations involving tensors that require gradients. Calling `loss.backward()` computes derivatives and stores gradients in leaf tensors such as trainable parameters. Gradients accumulate by default, so training loops normally clear them before the next backward pass.\n```pytorch\nw = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\nloss = (w - 5) ** 2\nloss.backward()\nprint(w.grad)  # derivative: 2 * (w - 5)\n```\nThe derivative tells how the loss changes with a small parameter change. It is not the updated weight; the optimizer applies a parameter update separately. A missing gradient can signal that a tensor was detached, the path did not contribute to the loss, or the graph was not built.\nCHECK: What does `loss.backward()` do for a scalar loss? ;; Computes gradients through the recorded graph ;; Automatically updates every parameter ;; Clears gradients before computing ;; Backward computes derivatives; optimizers perform updates\n\n## Gradient accumulation and debugging\nGradients accumulate into `.grad`, which is useful for gradient accumulation across mini-batches but surprising if unintended. Standard loops call `optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)`, compute predictions and loss, call `backward()`, then `optimizer.step()`. Use `torch.no_grad()` or `torch.inference_mode()` for evaluation, not for the training forward pass that needs gradients.\n```pytorch\noptimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\nprediction = model(inputs)\nloss = loss_fn(prediction, targets)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\n```\nInspect for `None`, NaN, or exploding gradients. Gradient clipping can help some unstable recurrent or deep models, but it should not hide a wrong loss scale, bad normalization, or incorrect tensor shapes. Use `detach()` only when intentionally stopping gradient flow.\nCHECK: Why clear gradients before an ordinary training iteration? ;; PyTorch accumulates gradients by default ;; Backward clears old gradients automatically ;; Clearing gradients updates weights ;; Clearing avoids mixing the current gradient with previous batches unintentionally","PyTorch 3: nn.Module, Losses & Optimizers":"## Models, modules, and logits\nSubclass `torch.nn.Module` for reusable architectures; define trainable layers in `__init__` and describe the forward computation in `forward`. Calling `model(x)` invokes the module machinery and hooks, so use it instead of calling `model.forward(x)` directly. A classifier with three classes commonly outputs logits shaped `(batch, 3)` before softmax.\n```pytorch\nfrom torch import nn\nclass Classifier(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, features, classes):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(features, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, classes))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)\n```\nA module registers parameters and buffers so `.to(device)`, `state_dict()`, and optimizers can discover them. Store layers as attributes or in `ModuleList`/`Sequential`, not an ordinary list that hides their parameters from module registration.\nCHECK: Why call `super().__init__()` in a custom `nn.Module`? ;; It initializes PyTorch module registration behavior ;; It trains the model automatically ;; It chooses the loss function ;; Parameters and child modules need correct registration\n\n## Losses, optimizers, and a training step\nChoose the loss based on target semantics. `CrossEntropyLoss` expects raw class logits and integer class indices; it internally combines log-softmax and negative log likelihood. `BCEWithLogitsLoss` is commonly used for binary or multi-label classification with raw logits. Do not apply softmax before `CrossEntropyLoss` unless a separate use specifically needs probabilities.\n```pytorch\noptimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\nlogits = model(inputs)\nloss = loss_fn(logits, targets)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\n```\nTrack loss values, learning rate, and validation metrics. Save `model.state_dict()` for model weights and recreate the architecture before loading it; full training resumption also needs optimizer and scheduler state plus relevant metadata.\nCHECK: What should be passed to `CrossEntropyLoss` for classification? ;; Raw logits and integer class-index targets ;; Softmax probabilities and one-hot strings only ;; Class names as text ;; The loss expects logits and class indices for this common usage","PyTorch 4: DataLoaders, Validation & Overfitting":"## Data pipelines and batching\nA `Dataset` defines how one example is retrieved; a `DataLoader` handles batching, optional shuffling, worker processes, and collation. Keep input and target shapes consistent with the model and loss. Shuffle training data when order should not convey structure, but do not randomly reshuffle the validation set for each reported comparison unless there is a specific reason.\n```pytorch\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\ntrain_ds = TensorDataset(train_x, train_y)\ntrain_loader = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=64, shuffle=True)\n```\nFor large datasets, avoid loading everything into Python lists if the dataset can be streamed or indexed efficiently. When using multiple workers, account for platform start methods and keep dataset worker functions picklable where required.\nCHECK: What is the main role of a `DataLoader`? ;; Batch and iterate through dataset examples ;; Define the neural network layers ;; Compute the optimizer gradient ;; It combines a dataset with batching and iteration behavior\n\n## Validation, overfitting, and experiment hygiene\nTraining loss indicates fit to the training data; validation performance estimates generalization on held-out examples. Overfitting often appears when training loss improves while validation performance stalls or worsens. Keep the test set untouched until model choices are complete, because repeated test-set tuning leaks information into development decisions.\n```pytorch\nmodel.eval()\nwith torch.inference_mode():\n    logits = model(validation_inputs)\n```\n`model.eval()` switches behavior for modules such as dropout and batch normalization; inference mode disables autograd overhead. These do different jobs. Also call `model.train()` before the next training phase. Seed randomness when useful, but understand that determinism can vary by hardware and kernels.\nCHECK: Why use both `model.eval()` and `torch.inference_mode()` during evaluation? ;; One changes module behavior; the other disables autograd tracking ;; They are identical operations ;; One moves tensors to the GPU ;; Evaluation needs both behavior mode and gradient-mode control","PyTorch 5: CNNs, Transfer Learning & Reproducibility":"## CNN shape arithmetic\nA convolution learns local filters shared across image positions. For one spatial dimension, the output size is `floor((input + 2*padding - dilation*(kernel - 1) - 1) / stride + 1)`. Pooling reduces spatial resolution; channels represent learned feature maps. Track height and width at each stage rather than guessing from a diagram.\n```pytorch\nnn.Conv2d(in_channels=3, out_channels=32, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1)\n```\nThe common image tensor layout is `(batch, channels, height, width)`. Normalize inputs consistently with the model or pretrained weights. A normalization mismatch can damage performance even when every tensor shape is correct.\nCHECK: What does `out_channels` specify for `Conv2d`? ;; The number of learned output feature maps ;; The batch size ;; The input image height ;; Each output channel corresponds to a learned filter bank\n\n## Transfer learning and freezing layers\nTransfer learning starts with features learned on a larger dataset. Load pretrained weights, apply the expected image preprocessing, replace the final classification layer, and train the new head first. Freezing parameters with `requires_grad_(False)` avoids computing gradients for them; if later fine-tuning, unfreeze selected blocks with a suitably smaller learning rate.\n```pytorch\nfor param in model.parameters():\n    param.requires_grad = False\n# Replace the final classifier with a new layer matching your class count.\n# Then pass only trainable parameters to the optimizer.\n```\nUse weights and preprocessing from a documented source. Do not assume every pretrained model expects the same image size or channel normalization. Train/validation splitting must also avoid near-duplicate images crossing the split.\nCHECK: Why must preprocessing match a pretrained model’s expected transforms? ;; Its learned features assume a compatible input distribution ;; Preprocessing changes the model class name ;; It guarantees zero validation error ;; Incorrect normalization can shift inputs away from the training distribution\n\n## Reproducible checkpoints and final audit\nA reproducible run records configuration, dataset version and split, random seeds, library/hardware details, metrics, and checkpoints. Save a state dictionary, optimizer state when resuming, and metadata such as class names. Set seeds for Python, NumPy, and PyTorch when used; exact determinism can require additional backend flags and may reduce performance.\n```pytorch\ntorch.save({\"model\": model.state_dict(), \"classes\": class_names}, \"checkpoint.pt\")\n# Recreate the same architecture before loading the state dictionary.\n```\nBefore trusting a result, compare with a simple baseline, inspect class imbalance, examine errors by class, and verify there is no train/validation leakage. Strong engineering is part of machine learning, not a final cosmetic step.\nCHECK: What should a useful model checkpoint include beyond weights when needed? ;; Metadata such as class names and experiment configuration ;; Only a screenshot of the training curve ;; The entire test set in the model file ;; Metadata makes model reuse and interpretation safer"},"advanced":[{"t":"Algorithmic Complexity & Amortized Analysis","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"Big-O Big-Theta Big-Omega amortized analysis invariants","n":"Analyze the cost of operations, not just whether code returns the right answer. Learn asymptotic bounds, amortized cost, and how to communicate tradeoffs.","ex":"values = list(range(1000))\nlookup = set(values)\nprint(999 in values, 999 in lookup)","lesson":"## Asymptotic growth\nBig-O describes an upper growth bound as input size grows; it does not tell you the wall-clock time for one particular machine. Common classes are O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), and O(2ⁿ). For nested loops, count how many times the body actually runs; a loop inside a loop is not automatically quadratic if the inner range shrinks geometrically.\n```python\n# O(n): each item is inspected once\ndef contains(items, target):\n    for item in items:\n        if item == target:\n            return True\n    return False\n```\nSeparate worst-case, average-case, and best-case analysis. State the input assumptions, because a hash lookup, tree lookup, and list scan have different complexity and different constant factors.\nCHECK: What does O(n log n) usually tell you? ;; Growth is roughly proportional to n times log n ;; Runtime is exactly n seconds ;; It is always faster than O(n) ;; Big-O describes growth as input size increases\n\n## Amortized cost and space\nAmortized analysis averages the cost across a sequence of operations without relying on probabilistic input. A dynamic array append is usually O(1) amortized even though an occasional resize copies O(n) elements. Total cost over many appends is O(n), so average cost per append is constant.\n```python\nitems = []\nfor i in range(100_000):\n    items.append(i)  # amortized O(1) per append\n```\nSpace complexity includes auxiliary allocations, recursion stacks, and retained references. A solution that is asymptotically faster may need much more memory; for data pipelines, streaming often trades repeated computation for lower memory.\nCHECK: Why is list append usually called amortized O(1)? ;; Expensive resizes are spread across many cheap appends ;; Every append copies the whole list ;; Its cost is always exactly identical ;; The total resizing work across n appends is linear","q":[["What is the worst-case lookup cost for an unsorted Python list?",["O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)"],0,"A list scan may inspect every element."],["Dynamic-array append is generally…",["O(1) amortized","O(n) every time","O(log n)"],0,"Occasional resizes are spread across a sequence of appends."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `two_sum_indices(nums, target)` in O(n) expected time. Return indices of two different elements whose values sum to target, or None if no pair exists. Explain the time/space tradeoff in a comment.","s":"def two_sum_indices(nums, target):\n    pass\n","h":"Scan once. Store each value’s index in a dictionary; for the current value, check whether target - value has already been seen.","t":"assert two_sum_indices([2,7,11,15],9)==(0,1) or two_sum_indices([2,7,11,15],9)==[0,1],\"find indices for 2+7\"\nassert two_sum_indices([3,3],6) in ((0,1),[0,1]),\"the same index cannot be reused\"\nassert two_sum_indices([1,2],8) is None,\"return None when there is no pair\""},"d":{"p":"This duplicate detector reports duplicates for lists that contain only unique values. Fix it so each value is checked against prior values, not against the entire original list.","s":"def contains_duplicate(values):\n    for value in values:\n        if value in values:\n            return True\n    return False\n","h":"Track values already seen in a set. A set membership check is expected O(1) on average.","t":"assert contains_duplicate([1,2,3]) is False,\"unique values are not duplicates\"\nassert contains_duplicate([1,2,1]) is True,\"detect a repeated value\"\nassert contains_duplicate([]) is False,\"empty input has no duplicates\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Algorithmic Complexity & Amortized Analysis","p":"Build a `RunningStats` class that maintains count, total, minimum, and maximum as values arrive. `add(value)` must run in O(1); `summary()` returns a dict and handles an empty stream explicitly.","s":"class RunningStats:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.count = 0\n        self.total = 0\n        self.minimum = None\n        self.maximum = None\n\n    def add(self, value):\n        pass\n\n    def summary(self):\n        pass\n","h":"Update each field once per value. The first value initializes both min and max; never rescan prior inputs.","t":"r=RunningStats();r.add(4);r.add(-1);r.add(10)\nassert r.summary()[\"count\"]==3 and r.summary()[\"total\"]==13,\"track count and total\"\nassert r.summary()[\"minimum\"]==-1 and r.summary()[\"maximum\"]==10,\"track extrema incrementally\""}},{"t":"Recursion, Memoization & Stack Limits","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"recursion base case recurrence memoization call stack tail recursion","n":"Understand recursive correctness, recurrence relations, memoization, and why Python recursion is not a substitute for every loop.","ex":"from functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef ways(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)\nprint(ways(10))","lesson":"## Prove the base case and progress\nA recursive function needs a base case and a step that moves toward it. A useful proof has two parts: base cases are correct, and assuming smaller calls are correct, the current call produces the correct result. If a call does not reduce the problem, it may recurse forever until `RecursionError`.\n```python\ndef sum_to(n):\n    if n <= 0:\n        return 0\n    return n + sum_to(n - 1)\n```\nBefore writing recursion, define what each call promises to return. That contract makes the recurrence easier to reason about than tracing arbitrary stack frames.\nCHECK: What two ingredients does a well-founded recursive function need? ;; A correct base case and progress toward it ;; A global variable and a while loop ;; A decorator and a class ;; Each recursive path must eventually reach a base case\n\n## Memoization and stack depth\nMemoization saves results for repeated inputs. For Fibonacci-like recurrences, caching reduces exponential repeated work to O(n) distinct states, but recursive call depth remains O(n). An iterative dynamic program can use O(1) extra space when only a fixed number of previous states are needed.\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef fib(n):\n    if n < 2: return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n```\nDo not reflexively increase `sys.setrecursionlimit`: deeply recursive algorithms can exhaust the C stack or crash the process. If input depth is untrusted, an explicit stack or iterative algorithm is often safer.\nCHECK: What does memoization improve in a recurrence with repeated subproblems? ;; It avoids recomputing the same state ;; It eliminates every stack frame ;; It makes recursion tail-call optimized ;; Cached states can reduce repeated work while recursion depth may still remain","q":[["What does memoization cache?",["Results for previously seen inputs","The Python source file","Every stack frame forever"],0,"Memoization stores function results keyed by arguments."],["Does caching eliminate recursion depth?",["No","Yes, always","Only for integers"],0,"It reduces repeated work, not the number of nested calls on a path."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `count_paths(rows, cols)` for a grid where each move goes right or down. Return the number of paths using O(cols) extra space; return 0 if either dimension is non-positive.","s":"def count_paths(rows, cols):\n    pass\n","h":"Maintain one row of DP values. For each cell, the number of paths is the paths from above plus paths from the left.","t":"assert count_paths(1,5)==1 and count_paths(3,3)==6,\"count right/down paths\"\nassert count_paths(0,3)==0 and count_paths(3,-1)==0,\"non-positive dimensions have zero paths\""},"d":{"p":"The recursive sum has a wrong base case. Repair it so sum_to(n) returns 1 + 2 + ... + n and returns 0 for n <= 0.","s":"def sum_to(n):\n    if n <= 0:\n        return 1\n    return n + sum_to(n - 1)\n","h":"The base case should contribute the sum of an empty prefix: zero.","t":"assert sum_to(5)==15,\"sum from 1 through 5\"\nassert sum_to(1)==1 and sum_to(0)==0 and sum_to(-3)==0,\"base cases must be zero for n <= 0\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Recursion, Memoization & Stack Limits","p":"Implement iterative `fib(n)` that returns the nth Fibonacci number with O(1) extra space and rejects negative n with ValueError. Avoid recursive calls.","s":"def fib(n):\n    pass\n","h":"Keep two variables for consecutive values and update them in a loop.","t":"assert [fib(i) for i in range(8)]==[0,1,1,2,3,5,8,13],\"Fibonacci sequence is incorrect\"\ntry:\n    fib(-1)\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"negative input should raise ValueError\")"}},{"t":"Dynamic Programming & State Design","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"state transition recurrence memoization tabulation reconstruction","n":"Learn how to turn an exponential search into a set of overlapping subproblems with precise state definitions and transitions.","ex":"def min_coins(coins, amount):\n    dp = [0] + [float(\"inf\")] * amount\n    for a in range(1, amount + 1):\n        dp[a] = min((dp[a-c] + 1 for c in coins if c <= a), default=float(\"inf\"))\n    return dp[amount] if dp[amount] != float(\"inf\") else None\nprint(min_coins([1, 3, 4], 6))","lesson":"## Define a state before a loop\nDynamic programming (DP) works when a problem can be decomposed into overlapping subproblems and an optimal answer can be built from smaller answers. State what `dp[i]` means in one sentence before coding. Then define the base cases and transition formula; code is just a schedule for evaluating that recurrence.\n```python\n# dp[a] = fewest coins needed to make amount a\n# dp[0] = 0; dp[a] = min(dp[a - coin] + 1)\n```\nA state that omits necessary information leads to incorrect reuse. For example, bounded coin use may require tracking both amount and which item index is allowed next.\nCHECK: What should you define first when designing DP? ;; The exact meaning of each state ;; The variable names in the final loop ;; A random cache size ;; A state definition determines what each stored value guarantees\n\n## Tabulation, memory, and reconstruction\nTop-down DP uses recursion and memoization; bottom-up DP fills a table in dependency order. Space can sometimes be compressed when a new row depends only on a small number of previous rows. But compression must preserve update order: iterating forward versus backward can change whether an item is reused in a knapsack problem.\n```python\n# For 0/1 knapsack, walk capacity downward\nfor weight, value in items:\n    for cap in range(limit, weight - 1, -1):\n        dp[cap] = max(dp[cap], dp[cap-weight] + value)\n```\nIf you need an actual solution rather than its score, keep parent decisions or reconstruct by checking which transition produced the stored value.\nCHECK: Why does 0/1 knapsack usually iterate capacity downward for each item? ;; To avoid using the same item multiple times in the same pass ;; To sort the weights ;; To avoid storing a DP array ;; Downward traversal prevents current-item updates from being reused","q":[["What does `dp[a]` mean in this example?",["Fewest coins needed to make amount a","The value of coin a","The number of input coins"],0,"A clear state sentence makes the recurrence meaningful."],["What must be preserved when compressing DP memory?",["All information needed by future transitions","Every old table cell","Only the array name"],0,"Memory optimization is valid only if the transition dependencies remain available."]],"x":{"p":"Write `max_non_adjacent(values)` for the largest sum of non-adjacent values. It may choose no elements, so an empty list returns 0. Use O(1) auxiliary space.","s":"def max_non_adjacent(values):\n    pass\n","h":"At each item, compare skipping it against taking it plus the best sum through two positions earlier.","t":"assert max_non_adjacent([2,7,9,3,1])==12,\"choose 2+9+1\"\nassert max_non_adjacent([])==0 and max_non_adjacent([-3,-1])==0,\"empty choice is allowed\""},"d":{"p":"This coin-change DP crashes when no coin can contribute to an amount. Make it return None when the amount cannot be formed and 0 for amount zero.","s":"def min_coins(coins, amount):\n    dp = [0] + [float(\"inf\")] * amount\n    for a in range(1, amount + 1):\n        dp[a] = min(dp[a-c] + 1 for c in coins if c <= a)\n    return dp[amount] if dp[amount] != float(\"inf\") else None\n","h":"Use a default when the generator of usable coins is empty; keep unreachable states at infinity.","t":"assert min_coins([1,3,4],6)==2,\"6 can be formed as 3+3\"\nassert min_coins([5],3) is None,\"amount 3 cannot be formed with coin 5\"\nassert min_coins([1,2],0)==0,\"zero amount needs zero coins\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Dynamic Programming & State Design","p":"Implement `edit_distance(a, b)` (Levenshtein distance: insert, delete, replace) with O(len(a)*len(b)) time and only O(min(len(a),len(b))) auxiliary space.","s":"def edit_distance(a, b):\n    pass\n","h":"Each state is the minimum operations to transform prefixes. Use a rolling row and initialize the empty-prefix distances.","t":"assert edit_distance(\"kitten\",\"sitting\")==3,\"kitten to sitting takes three edits\"\nassert edit_distance(\"\",\"abc\")==3 and edit_distance(\"same\",\"same\")==0,\"empty and identical strings\""}},{"t":"Data Structures: Stacks, Queues & Heaps","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"deque heapq monotonic stack FIFO LIFO priority queue","n":"Choose a structure from the operations and invariants a problem requires.","ex":"from collections import deque\nfrom heapq import heappush, heappop\nqueue = deque([\"a\", \"b\"])\nqueue.append(\"c\")\nprint(queue.popleft())\nheap = []\nfor priority, task in [(5,\"low\"),(1,\"urgent\"),(3,\"normal\")]:\n    heappush(heap, (priority, task))\nprint(heappop(heap))","lesson":"## Stack versus queue versus heap\nA stack is last-in, first-out; a queue is first-in, first-out; a priority queue returns the item with the best priority. A Python list is a good stack with `append`/`pop`; `collections.deque` is a good queue; `heapq` implements a min-heap. A heap is not fully sorted—only its root is guaranteed to be the smallest item.\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nstack = [1, 2]; stack.append(3); print(stack.pop())\nqueue = deque([1, 2]); queue.append(3); print(queue.popleft())\n```\nState the invariant clearly: a stack remembers nesting, a queue preserves arrival order, and a heap prioritizes the next item.\nCHECK: Which data structure is the natural choice for retrieving the smallest-priority item repeatedly? ;; A heap / priority queue ;; A set with no ordering ;; A list using `pop(0)` every time ;; `heapq` maintains the smallest item at its root\n\n## Monotonic stacks and lazy deletion\nA monotonic stack keeps values increasing or decreasing to answer next-greater-element and span-style questions in linear time. Each item is pushed and popped at most once. For heaps with changing priorities, Python's `heapq` does not offer a decrease-key operation; common strategies include pushing a new entry with a version and ignoring stale entries when popped.\n```python\ndef next_greater(values):\n    out = [-1] * len(values)\n    stack = []\n    for i, value in enumerate(values):\n        while stack and values[stack[-1]] < value:\n            out[stack.pop()] = value\n        stack.append(i)\n    return out\n```\nThe proof often comes from accounting: once an index is popped, it never returns, so nested `while` loops can still total O(n) work.\nCHECK: Why can a monotonic-stack algorithm with a nested `while` loop still be O(n)? ;; Each index is pushed and popped at most once ;; The inner loop always runs exactly once ;; Python optimizes all loops ;; Amortized counting bounds total stack operations","q":[["What does a min-heap guarantee?",["The smallest item is at the root","Every array element is sorted","The largest item is at the root"],0,"A heap partially orders data around its root."],["What does a queue remove first?",["The oldest enqueued item","The newest item","The smallest item by value"],0,"FIFO means first in, first out."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `next_greater(values)` returning, for each value, the first strictly greater value to its right, or -1. Target O(n) using a monotonic stack.","s":"def next_greater(values):\n    pass\n","h":"Keep indices whose next greater value has not been found. When a larger value arrives, resolve the previous smaller values.","t":"assert next_greater([2,1,3,2,4])==[3,3,4,4,-1],\"next greater values are incorrect\"\nassert next_greater([])==[],\"empty input\""},"d":{"p":"k_largest returns the smallest values instead. Correct the heap operation and keep the result sorted from largest to smallest.","s":"import heapq\ndef k_largest(values, k):\n    return heapq.nsmallest(k, values)\n","h":"heapq has separate helpers for the k smallest and k largest values. Sort direction is part of this function’s contract.","t":"assert k_largest([5,1,9,3,7],3)==[9,7,5],\"return the three largest descending\"\nassert k_largest([2,1],0)==[],\"k=0 returns an empty list\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Data Structures: Stacks, Queues & Heaps","p":"Build a `TaskQueue` wrapping `heapq`. `push(priority, task)` inserts an item, `pop()` returns the lowest-priority task, and equal priorities must be stable in insertion order.","s":"import heapq\nclass TaskQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.heap = []\n        self.sequence = 0\n    def push(self, priority, task):\n        pass\n    def pop(self):\n        pass\n","h":"Store tuples `(priority, sequence, task)` so Python never needs to compare task objects to break a tie.","t":"q=TaskQueue();q.push(2,\"first\");q.push(1,\"urgent\");q.push(2,\"second\")\nassert [q.pop(),q.pop(),q.pop()]==[\"urgent\",\"first\",\"second\"],\"priority then insertion order\""}},{"t":"Graph Algorithms: BFS, DFS & Dijkstra","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"adjacency list BFS DFS visited shortest path Dijkstra","n":"Model relationships as graphs, choose a traversal, and prove what the traversal discovers.","ex":"from collections import deque\ndef distances(graph, start):\n    out = {start: 0}\n    todo = deque([start])\n    while todo:\n        node = todo.popleft()\n        for nxt in graph.get(node, []):\n            if nxt not in out:\n                out[nxt] = out[node] + 1\n                todo.append(nxt)\n    return out\nprint(distances({\"A\":[\"B\",\"C\"],\"B\":[\"D\"],\"C\":[],\"D\":[]}, \"A\"))","lesson":"## Represent a graph\nA graph contains vertices and edges; edges may be directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. An adjacency list maps each vertex to its neighbors and stores O(V + E) data. An adjacency matrix uses O(V²) space but makes checking whether a particular edge exists constant-time.\n```python\ngraph = {\"A\": [\"B\", \"C\"], \"B\": [\"A\"], \"C\": [\"A\"]}\n```\nDecide whether missing vertices should behave as having no neighbors or trigger an error. That is a data-model contract, not merely an implementation detail.\nCHECK: When is an adjacency list especially useful? ;; Sparse graphs where storing every possible edge would waste space ;; Every possible pair has an edge ;; You need O(1) indexing by matrix coordinate ;; It stores only actual neighbors for a sparse graph\n\n## BFS, DFS, and weighted shortest paths\nBreadth-first search (BFS) explores by distance layers and finds shortest paths in an unweighted graph. Depth-first search (DFS) explores deeply and is useful for reachability, cycle detection, and topological algorithms. Mark nodes visited when enqueuing or pushing so cycles do not repeatedly add them.\n```python\n# BFS: queue contains the current frontier\n# Dijkstra: priority queue contains tentative (distance, vertex) pairs\n```\nDijkstra requires non-negative edge weights. With negative edges, use another algorithm such as Bellman–Ford when its assumptions fit. When paths matter, store a predecessor map and reconstruct the route by walking backward from the destination.\nCHECK: Why does ordinary BFS find shortest paths in an unweighted graph? ;; It visits nodes in increasing edge-distance layers ;; It always explores the alphabetically first node ;; It sorts all edges by weight ;; FIFO ordering ensures every shallower frontier is processed first","q":[["Which algorithm finds shortest paths by edge count?",["BFS","DFS","Binary search"],0,"BFS expands in layers of increasing distance."],["Which Dijkstra assumption matters?",["All edge weights are non-negative","The graph is a tree","Every vertex has two neighbors"],0,"Negative edges invalidate Dijkstra's usual correctness guarantee."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `reachable(graph, start, target)` using iterative BFS or DFS. Return False when the target is disconnected; handle a missing start as an isolated node.","s":"from collections import deque\ndef reachable(graph, start, target):\n    pass\n","h":"Keep a visited set and a worklist; return as soon as the target is discovered.","t":"g={\"a\":[\"b\"],\"b\":[\"c\"],\"x\":[]}\nassert reachable(g,\"a\",\"c\") is True and reachable(g,\"a\",\"x\") is False,\"reachability is incorrect\"\nassert reachable({},\"same\",\"same\") is True,\"a node reaches itself\""},"d":{"p":"This breadth-first distance function starts every route at distance 1, so a node has distance 1 from itself. Fix the off-by-one bug.","s":"from collections import deque\ndef shortest_distance(graph, start, goal):\n    queue = deque([(start, 1)])\n    seen = {start}\n    while queue:\n        node, distance = queue.popleft()\n        if node == goal:\n            return distance\n        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):\n            if neighbor not in seen:\n                seen.add(neighbor)\n                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))\n    return None\n","h":"The starting vertex is zero edges away from itself. BFS adds one per traversed edge.","t":"graph={\"a\":[\"b\"],\"b\":[\"a\",\"c\"],\"c\":[\"b\"]}\nassert shortest_distance(graph,\"a\",\"a\")==0,\"same vertex has distance zero\"\nassert shortest_distance(graph,\"a\",\"c\")==2,\"a to c uses two edges\"\nassert shortest_distance(graph,\"a\",\"z\") is None,\"unreachable node returns None\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Graph Algorithms: BFS, DFS & Dijkstra","p":"Write `shortest_path(graph, start, goal)` for an unweighted adjacency list. Return a list of vertices in the shortest route, including both endpoints, or None if unreachable.","s":"from collections import deque\ndef shortest_path(graph, start, goal):\n    pass\n","h":"BFS plus a parent map reconstructs the route. Handle start == goal before exploring.","t":"g={\"a\":[\"b\",\"c\"],\"b\":[\"d\"],\"c\":[\"d\"],\"d\":[],\"z\":[]}\nassert shortest_path(g,\"a\",\"a\")==[\"a\"] and len(shortest_path(g,\"a\",\"d\"))==3,\"route should use two edges\"\nassert shortest_path(g,\"a\",\"z\") is None,\"unreachable goal returns None\""}},{"t":"Greedy Algorithms & Correctness","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"greedy choice exchange argument interval scheduling prefix","n":"Greedy algorithms make a locally best choice. Learn when this works, how to justify it, and how to detect cases that require DP.","ex":"def select_intervals(intervals):\n    chosen, end = [], float(\"-inf\")\n    for start, finish in sorted(intervals, key=lambda p: p[1]):\n        if start >= end:\n            chosen.append((start, finish))\n            end = finish\n    return chosen\nprint(select_intervals([(1,3),(2,5),(4,6),(6,8)]))","lesson":"## Greedy choice needs proof\nA greedy algorithm commits to a local choice without reconsidering it. Sorting intervals by end time maximizes the number of non-overlapping intervals because an interval that finishes earliest leaves at least as much room for future choices. A correctness argument is needed; a plausible intuition alone is not proof.\n```python\nintervals = [(1, 4), (3, 5), (4, 7)]\nby_finish = sorted(intervals, key=lambda pair: pair[1])\n```\nExchange arguments show that an optimal solution can be transformed to include the greedy choice without making the solution worse. This is a common proof technique for scheduling and spanning-tree algorithms.\nCHECK: What does an exchange argument establish? ;; An optimal solution can be changed to include the greedy choice without worsening it ;; Every greedy algorithm is correct ;; The code uses less memory ;; It connects a local choice to an optimal solution\n\n## Recognize greedy counterexamples\nCoin change is not greedy-correct for every currency system. With coins 1, 3, and 4, choosing the largest coin first for amount 6 gives 4 + 1 + 1, while 3 + 3 uses fewer coins. This counterexample shows why a general claim needs evidence or proof.\n```python\ncoins = [1, 3, 4]\n# For amount 6: greedy gives [4, 1, 1], optimum is [3, 3].\n```\nA good algorithm workflow is to test small counterexamples, identify the required property, and then prove the claim. For optimization problems, compare candidate greedy choices against DP on small generated cases.\nCHECK: What does the coin set 1, 3, 4 demonstrate for amount 6? ;; Largest-coin-first is not always optimal ;; Greedy is always better than DP ;; Dynamic programming cannot solve change ;; A counterexample disproves the universal greedy claim","q":[["Why sort intervals by finishing time?",["The earliest finish leaves the most room for future intervals","It guarantees alphabetical order","It makes every interval non-overlap"],0,"This is the key greedy choice for maximum-cardinality interval scheduling."],["What is a counterexample useful for?",["Disproving a claim that an algorithm always works","Proving every possible input works","Making runtime constant"],0,"One valid counterexample defeats a universal correctness claim."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `max_activities(intervals)` returning the maximum number of compatible intervals. Intervals are `(start, finish)` and touching endpoints are allowed. Use the proven earliest-finish greedy strategy.","s":"def max_activities(intervals):\n    pass\n","h":"Sort by finish time, then accept an interval if its start is at least the previous accepted finish.","t":"assert max_activities([(1,3),(2,4),(3,5),(5,7)])==3,\"three compatible intervals can be chosen\"\nassert max_activities([])==0,\"empty input gives zero\""},"d":{"p":"The interval scheduler sorts by starting time, which can choose a long interval and block many short compatible ones. Sort by finishing time instead.","s":"def select_intervals(intervals):\n    ordered = sorted(intervals, key=lambda pair: pair[0])\n    chosen = []\n    end = float(\"-inf\")\n    for start, finish in ordered:\n        if start >= end:\n            chosen.append((start, finish))\n            end = finish\n    return chosen\n","h":"For maximum-cardinality interval scheduling, take the compatible interval that finishes earliest.","t":"items=[(1,10),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5),(5,6)]\nassert select_intervals(items)==[(2,3),(3,4),(4,5),(5,6)],\"choose four short compatible intervals\"\nassert select_intervals([])==[],\"empty schedule\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Greedy Algorithms & Correctness","p":"Implement `min_platforms(times)` for train intervals `(arrival, departure)`. Return the maximum number of trains simultaneously present. You may sort arrivals and departures separately and use two pointers.","s":"def min_platforms(times):\n    pass\n","h":"The answer increases when an arrival occurs before the earliest departure; otherwise a platform is freed.","t":"assert min_platforms([(1,4),(2,5),(7,9)])==2,\"two simultaneous trains at peak\"\nassert min_platforms([])==0,\"no trains need no platforms\""}},{"t":"Sorting, Searching & Hash Table Internals","track":"Algorithms & Systems","b":"stable sort binary search hash collision load factor Timsort","n":"Analyze ordering, maintain search invariants, and understand the expected-versus-worst-case tradeoffs behind built-in containers.","ex":"def lower_bound(values, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(values)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if values[mid] < target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\nprint(lower_bound([1,2,2,2,5], 2))","lesson":"## Stable sorting and binary search\nPython's built-in sort is stable: values with equal keys retain their input order. A key function is evaluated to derive ordering; it is usually cleaner than repeatedly computing attributes inside comparisons. Binary search needs sorted data and a precise interval invariant. `bisect_left` finds the first insertion point that keeps the sequence sorted.\n```python\nfrom bisect import bisect_left\nvalues = [1, 2, 2, 2, 5]\ni = bisect_left(values, 2)\nprint(i)  # 1\n```\nThe insertion point is not always a found index: check bounds and equality. Binary search reduces the candidate range geometrically, so it takes O(log n) comparisons on a random-access sorted sequence.\nCHECK: What does `bisect_left` return when the target is absent? ;; The index where it can be inserted while preserving order ;; Always -1 ;; The nearest value itself ;; It returns an insertion position rather than a membership result\n\n## Hashing, collisions, and contracts\nA dictionary maps hashable keys to values. Hash tables use a hash to narrow the search, then equality checks to resolve collisions. Average lookup and insertion are O(1), but adversarial collisions can worsen performance. The key contract is that equal objects must have equal hashes and a key's hash should not change while stored.\n```python\ncache = {(\"model\", 3): \"ready\"}\nprint(cache.get((\"model\", 3)))\n```\nDo not derive security guarantees from a single hash function detail. Python randomizes hashes for some types, but this is not a reason to accept unbounded user-controlled workloads without input limits.\nCHECK: What must be true for two keys that compare equal? ;; They must have the same hash ;; They must have different hashes ;; Their repr strings must match ;; Hash tables depend on compatible equality and hash behavior","q":[["How many comparisons does binary search need asymptotically?",["O(log n)","O(n²)","O(1) for every input"],0,"Each step halves the remaining candidate interval."],["What is the key hash contract?",["Equal keys must have equal hashes","Every object has a unique hash","Hashes are the same as repr strings"],0,"Hash lookup uses hashing and equality together."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `first_index(values, target)` for a sorted list containing duplicates. Return the first index of target or -1. Use binary search, not `list.index`.","s":"def first_index(values, target):\n    pass\n","h":"When the middle value matches, save its index and continue searching the left half.","t":"assert first_index([1,2,2,2,5],2)==1,\"return the first duplicate\"\nassert first_index([],3)==-1 and first_index([1,4],2)==-1,\"missing target returns -1\""},"d":{"p":"This binary search mixes half-open bounds with an inclusive-style update and can miss present values. Keep the search interval [lo, hi) consistent.","s":"def binary_search(values, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(values)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if values[mid] < target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        elif values[mid] > target:\n            hi = mid - 1\n        else:\n            return mid\n    return -1\n","h":"For the half-open interval [lo, hi), when values[mid] is too large set hi = mid.","t":"values=[1,3,5,7,9]\nassert binary_search(values,3)==1,\"find an element after the first item\"\nassert binary_search(values,1)==0,\"find the first item\"\nassert binary_search(values,4)==-1,\"missing values return -1\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Sorting, Searching & Hash Table Internals","p":"Write `stable_group_sort(records)` where each record is `(group, score, label)`. Sort by group ascending and score descending while preserving input order for records with equal group and score.","s":"def stable_group_sort(records):\n    pass\n","h":"Python sort is stable; a key of `(group, -score)` is sufficient.","t":"rows=[(\"b\",2,\"first\"),(\"a\",1,\"x\"),(\"b\",2,\"second\")]\nassert stable_group_sort(rows)==[(\"a\",1,\"x\"),(\"b\",2,\"first\"),(\"b\",2,\"second\")],\"equal-key records preserve their order\""}},{"t":"Python Protocols & Structural Typing","track":"Python Internals","b":"Protocol runtime_checkable generic variance ABC duck typing","n":"Design reusable interfaces based on behavior rather than inheritance. Learn protocols, generic APIs, and the limits of runtime type checks.","ex":"from typing import Protocol\nclass SupportsClose(Protocol):\n    def close(self) -> None: ...\ndef shut_down(resource: SupportsClose) -> None:\n    resource.close()\nprint(\"Structural types describe shape, not ancestry.\")","lesson":"## Nominal and structural typing\nNominal typing asks whether a value belongs to a declared class or subclass. Structural typing asks whether it provides the required operations. Python duck typing has long used the structural idea at runtime; `typing.Protocol` lets static type checkers express those expectations explicitly.\n```python\nfrom typing import Protocol\nclass HasLength(Protocol):\n    def __len__(self) -> int: ...\ndef describe(value: HasLength) -> str:\n    return f\"length={len(value)}\"\n```\nA protocol describes the minimum behavior a consumer needs. It reduces coupling: a class can satisfy the interface without importing or inheriting from the module that defines it.\nCHECK: What is the main purpose of `Protocol`? ;; Describe required behavior without requiring explicit inheritance ;; Force every class to inherit from one base class ;; Validate all values at runtime ;; Structural typing focuses on available operations\n\n## Generics, variance, and API boundaries\nGeneric types preserve relationships between inputs and outputs, such as a function mapping a list of T to a list of T. Variance matters when substituting container types: a read-only producer can safely be more specific in some contexts, while mutable containers are generally invariant because they are both read and written.\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable\ndef first(items: Iterable[str]) -> str | None:\n    return next(iter(items), None)\n```\nUse `Iterable[T]` if you only need iteration, `Sequence[T]` if you need length and indexing, and `Mapping[K, V]` if you only read by key. Narrow requirements make your API more reusable.\nCHECK: When should a parameter be typed as `Iterable[T]` instead of `list[T]`? ;; When the function only needs to iterate ;; Whenever it will mutate list elements ;; When it needs random indexing guaranteed ;; A smaller contract admits generators, tuples, and lists","q":[["Does a class have to inherit from a Protocol to satisfy it statically?",["No, compatible methods can be enough","Yes, always","Only built-in classes can"],0,"Protocols enable structural typing."],["Which annotation is least restrictive for read-only iteration?",["Iterable[T]","list[T] only","dict[str, T] only"],0,"Choose the narrowest interface that provides needed operations."]],"x":{"p":"Implement a generic-style `first_or_none(items)` using only the iterator protocol. It returns the first value or None for an empty iterable, without requiring a list or indexing.","s":"def first_or_none(items):\n    pass\n","h":"Call `iter(items)` and then `next(iterator, None)`.","t":"assert first_or_none(iter([4,5]))==4 and first_or_none(()) is None,\"support iterators and empty iterables\"\nassert first_or_none(\"abc\")==\"a\",\"strings are iterable too\""},"d":{"p":"emit assumes the sink is a list and calls append, but the required interface is a file-like object with write(text). Fix emit to use that protocol.","s":"def emit(sink, text):\n    return sink.append(text)\n\nclass TextSink:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.data = \"\"\n    def write(self, text):\n        self.data += text\n        return len(text)\n\nsink = TextSink()\nresult = emit(sink, \"hello\")\n","h":"Structural typing cares whether an object offers the required operation; call write rather than requiring list-specific append.","t":"assert result==5,\"write returns the number of characters\"\nassert sink.data==\"hello\",\"text reached the sink\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Python Protocols & Structural Typing","p":"Create a protocol-like adapter function `close_all(resources)` that accepts any iterable of objects with a callable `close` attribute, closes them in order, and raises TypeError for an unsupported object.","s":"def close_all(resources):\n    pass\n","h":"Use `getattr(obj, \"close\", None)` and check callable before invoking it.","t":"class R:\n    def __init__(self): self.closed=False\n    def close(self): self.closed=True\na,b=R(),R();close_all([a,b])\nassert a.closed and b.closed,\"close every resource\"\ntry: close_all([object()])\nexcept TypeError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"missing close method must be rejected\")"}},{"t":"Descriptors & Attribute Lookup","track":"Python Internals","b":"__get__ __set__ __delete__ property data descriptor non-data descriptor","n":"Understand how attribute access, descriptors, properties, and instance dictionaries cooperate.","ex":"class Positive:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name): self.name = name\n    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):\n        if obj is None: return self\n        return obj.__dict__.get(self.name, 0)\n    def __set__(self, obj, value):\n        if value < 0: raise ValueError(\"must be non-negative\")\n        obj.__dict__[self.name] = value\nclass Sample:\n    score = Positive()\ns = Sample(); s.score = 5\nprint(s.score)","lesson":"## The descriptor protocol\nAn object defining `__get__`, `__set__`, or `__delete__` can control attribute access when stored on a class. A descriptor with `__set__` or `__delete__` is a data descriptor; a descriptor with only `__get__` is non-data. Data descriptors generally take precedence over the instance dictionary, which is why a `property` can validate assignment.\n```python\nclass Celsius:\n    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):\n        return obj.__dict__.get(\"_c\", 0)\n    def __set__(self, obj, value):\n        if value < -273.15: raise ValueError(\"below absolute zero\")\n        obj.__dict__[\"_c\"] = value\n```\nDescriptors power properties, methods, ORM fields, and many frameworks. You can often use `property` rather than writing a descriptor directly; custom descriptors shine when the behavior is reused across many attributes.\nCHECK: Which kind of descriptor generally wins over an instance dictionary entry? ;; A data descriptor ;; A local variable ;; Any plain integer ;; Data descriptors define assignment or deletion behavior\n\n## Attribute lookup details\nWhen `obj.name` is evaluated, Python applies type-level lookup rules, descriptor methods, and the instance dictionary in a defined order. Functions stored on a class are non-data descriptors; access through an instance creates a bound method with the instance supplied as `self`. `__set_name__` lets a descriptor learn the attribute name during class creation.\n```python\nclass Field:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.name = name\n```\nThis power has tradeoffs: descriptors are shared class objects, so per-instance values belong on the instance. Do not store one instance's mutable runtime state directly on the descriptor unless sharing is intentional.\nCHECK: Why should ordinary per-instance descriptor data usually live in `obj.__dict__` or equivalent storage? ;; The descriptor object is shared by all instances of the class ;; Every instance has a unique class descriptor ;; Descriptors cannot read instance attributes ;; Class-level descriptors are shared across instances","q":[["What does `__set_name__` commonly provide to a descriptor?",["The attribute name assigned in the owning class","The current instance value","The Python process ID"],0,"It runs during class creation for descriptors placed in the class body."],["Where should different instances store their own score values?",["In instance-specific storage","On one shared descriptor field","In the source file"],0,"The descriptor is shared; stored values should be per instance."]],"x":{"p":"Implement a `Validated` descriptor that accepts only integers from 0 through 100 inclusive, stores values per instance, and raises ValueError otherwise.","s":"class Validated:\n    pass\nclass Record:\n    score = Validated()\n","h":"Use `__set_name__`, store under a private key in `obj.__dict__`, and implement `__get__` plus `__set__`.","t":"a,b=Record(),Record();a.score=75\nassert a.score==75 and b.score is None,\"values are per instance\"\nfor bad in (-1,101,1.5):\n    try: a.score=bad\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"invalid values must be rejected\")"},"d":{"p":"This descriptor stores the value under one key but reads it under another. Make its storage consistent and keep the positive-number validation.","s":"class PositiveField:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):\n        if obj is None: return self\n        return obj.__dict__.get(self.name)\n    def __set__(self, obj, value):\n        if value <= 0: raise ValueError(\"must be positive\")\n        obj.__dict__[\"_\" + self.name] = value\n\nclass Account:\n    balance = PositiveField()\n","h":"Choose either name or _name and use the exact same key in __get__ and __set__.","t":"a=Account(); a.balance=12\nassert a.balance==12,\"descriptor should return the stored balance\"\ntry:\n    a.balance=0\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"non-positive balances must be rejected\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Descriptors & Attribute Lookup","p":"Implement a reusable `CachedProperty` descriptor that computes a method once per instance and caches the result on that instance. Replacing an instance value later should override the descriptor result.","s":"class CachedProperty:\n    def __init__(self, fn): self.fn = fn\n    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):\n        pass\n","h":"Return the descriptor from class-level access. On instance access, compute and write the value into the instance dictionary.","t":"class Data:\n    calls=0\n    @CachedProperty\n    def value(self):\n        self.calls+=1; return 42\nd=Data()\nassert d.value==42 and d.value==42 and d.calls==1,\"compute once per instance\"\nd.value=7\nassert d.value==7,\"instance override should work\""}},{"t":"Metaclasses & Class Creation","track":"Python Internals","b":"type metaclass __new__ __init_subclass__ class decorator registry","n":"Learn how Python creates classes and when metaclasses are justified over simpler customization hooks.","ex":"class RequireName:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        if not getattr(cls, \"name\", None):\n            raise TypeError(\"subclasses must define name\")\nclass Plugin(RequireName):\n    name = \"sample\"\nprint(Plugin.name)","lesson":"## Classes are created too\nMost classes are instances of `type`. A metaclass customizes class creation, often by controlling the namespace or validating attributes as the class is defined. For many ordinary use cases, a class decorator or `__init_subclass__` is simpler and easier to explain than a custom metaclass.\n```python\nclass RegistryBase:\n    registry = {}\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        key = getattr(cls, \"kind\", None)\n        if key: RegistryBase.registry[key] = cls\n```\nKeep registration behavior explicit and deterministic. If imports trigger plugin discovery, document that side effect and avoid surprising network or filesystem work at import time.\nCHECK: What simpler hook can often validate or register subclasses without a metaclass? ;; `__init_subclass__` ;; `__del__` ;; `__iter__` ;; It runs when a subclass is defined\n\n## Metaclass tradeoffs\nA metaclass can customize `__prepare__`, class namespace construction, and the resulting class object, but it adds a new layer to inheritance and debugging. Multiple metaclasses can conflict, and frameworks using one can be difficult to compose. Prefer a metaclass only when the behavior truly belongs to class creation and cannot be expressed cleanly otherwise.\n```python\nclass UpperAttrs(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        cleaned = {k.upper() if not k.startswith(\"__\") else k: v\n                   for k, v in namespace.items()}\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, cleaned)\n```\nIn production code, avoiding such namespace transformations is often wise because they obscure attribute names and frustrate tools. Powerful does not mean maintainable.\nCHECK: What is a sound reason to avoid a custom metaclass? ;; A simpler class hook solves the requirement clearly ;; Python cannot inherit from metaclasses ;; Metaclasses cannot inspect namespaces ;; Complex class creation machinery increases coupling and debugging cost","q":[["What is the default metaclass for most Python classes?",["type","object.__new__","property"],0,"The built-in type creates class objects."],["When does `__init_subclass__` run?",["When a subclass is defined","Every time an instance is accessed","Only when the program exits"],0,"It provides a subclass customization hook."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `Plugin.__init_subclass__` so subclasses with a non-empty class attribute `kind` are registered in a class-level mapping. Reject duplicate kinds with ValueError.","s":"class Plugin:\n    registry = {}\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):\n        pass\n","h":"Call super first, inspect `cls.kind`, and update the registry only after checking for duplicates.","t":"class JsonPlugin(Plugin): kind=\"json\"\nassert Plugin.registry[\"json\"] is JsonPlugin,\"register the subclass\"\ntry:\n    class DuplicatePlugin(Plugin): kind=\"json\"\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"duplicate plugin kind should fail\")"},"d":{"p":"RequireVersion is supposed to reject classes that do not define their own version attribute. It mistakenly checks the base classes instead of the namespace being created.","s":"class RequireVersion(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        if \"version\" in bases:\n            raise TypeError(\"define version on each class\")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\nclass Valid(metaclass=RequireVersion):\n    version = 1\n","h":"The attributes declared in the new class are in namespace, which is a mapping from names to values.","t":"assert Valid.version==1,\"keep an explicitly declared version\"\ntry:\n    class MissingVersion(metaclass=RequireVersion):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"classes without version must be rejected\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Metaclasses & Class Creation","p":"Create a `validated_record` class decorator that checks the class defines a callable `save` method and returns the original class unchanged. Raise TypeError at decoration time if the contract is missing.","s":"def validated_record(cls):\n    pass\n","h":"Use `callable(getattr(cls, \"save\", None))`; return `cls` after the check.","t":"@validated_record\nclass Good:\n    def save(self): pass\nassert Good.__name__==\"Good\",\"decorator returns the original class\"\ntry:\n    @validated_record\n    class Bad: pass\nexcept TypeError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"missing save method must fail\")"}},{"t":"Memory, Garbage Collection, Copies & Weakrefs","track":"Python Internals","b":"reference counting cyclic GC copy deepcopy weakref memory lifetime","n":"Understand object lifetime, aliasing, cyclic references, and how to profile memory without guessing.","ex":"import copy\noriginal = {\"items\": [[1], [2]]}\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\noriginal[\"items\"][0].append(9)\nprint(shallow[\"items\"][0], deep[\"items\"][0])","lesson":"## References, copies, and cycles\nNames hold references to objects. A shallow copy duplicates the outer container but reuses nested references; a deep copy recursively copies many contained objects, subject to custom copy behavior and memoization for cycles. Neither is automatically the right semantic choice—sometimes a deliberate, targeted copy is better.\n```python\nimport copy\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\na[0].append(3)\n```\nCPython primarily uses reference counting plus a cyclic garbage collector for unreachable reference cycles. Do not rely on immediate finalization for external resources; use context managers to close files, sockets, and locks.\nCHECK: What does a shallow copy of a nested list preserve? ;; References to the same nested objects ;; Independent copies of every nested object ;; No references at all ;; Only the outer container is duplicated\n\n## Weak references and memory diagnosis\nA weak reference points to an object without keeping it alive solely because the weak reference exists. Weak-key dictionaries and weak-value dictionaries are useful for caches tied to object lifetime, but the referenced type must support weak references. Weakrefs do not fix a memory leak caused by a still-live strong reference.\n```python\nimport tracemalloc\ntracemalloc.start()\n# run a representative workload here\ncurrent, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\nprint(current, peak)\n```\nMeasure before optimizing. `tracemalloc` can identify allocations attributed to Python code; process RSS includes other native allocations. Releasing a reference makes an object eligible for collection, but memory allocators may keep released arenas for reuse rather than returning them to the OS immediately.\nCHECK: Does creating a weak reference usually keep the target object alive? ;; No, a weak reference does not itself keep the target alive ;; Yes, it is equivalent to a strong reference ;; Only if the object is a list ;; Weak references are meant to observe lifetime without owning it","q":[["Does `copy.copy` recursively clone nested lists?",["No, nested objects are shared","Yes, always","It removes nested objects"],0,"A shallow copy only duplicates the outer container."],["What is a weak reference for?",["Referencing an object without keeping it alive by itself","Preventing every garbage collection","Making an object immutable"],0,"Weak references do not own the target's lifetime."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `clone_config(config)` to copy a nested configuration dict so changes to nested lists and dicts in the clone do not affect the original. Use the appropriate standard-library operation.","s":"def clone_config(config):\n    pass\n","h":"For this contract, a recursive deep copy is appropriate.","t":"import copy\na={\"model\":{\"layers\":[1,2]},\"tags\":[\"x\"]};b=clone_config(a)\nb[\"model\"][\"layers\"].append(3);b[\"tags\"].append(\"y\")\nassert a=={\"model\":{\"layers\":[1,2]},\"tags\":[\"x\"]},\"clone mutations must not leak to original\""},"d":{"p":"clone_grid copies only the outer list, so writing to a nested row changes the original. Make a full independent copy.","s":"import copy\ndef clone_grid(grid):\n    result = copy.copy(grid)\n    result[0][0] = 0\n    return result\n","h":"A shallow copy shares nested lists. Use a recursive copy when nested containers must be independent.","t":"original=[[1,2],[3,4]]\ncloned=clone_grid(original)\nassert cloned==[[0,2],[3,4]],\"cloned first cell should be changed\"\nassert original==[[1,2],[3,4]],\"original nested rows must not change\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Memory, Garbage Collection, Copies & Weakrefs","p":"Write `merge_settings(defaults, overrides)` returning a recursive merged copy. Nested mappings merge by key; any non-mapping override replaces the corresponding default. Neither input may be mutated.","s":"def merge_settings(defaults, overrides):\n    pass\n","h":"Deep-copy the default mapping, then recursively merge when both old and new values are dicts.","t":"a={\"model\":{\"layers\":2,\"dropout\":0.1},\"seed\":1};b={\"model\":{\"layers\":4},\"seed\":9}\nr=merge_settings(a,b)\nassert r=={\"model\":{\"layers\":4,\"dropout\":0.1},\"seed\":9},\"nested overrides merge\"\nassert a[\"model\"][\"layers\"]==2 and b[\"model\"][\"layers\"]==4,\"inputs stay unchanged\""}},{"t":"Threads, Processes & the GIL","track":"Python Internals","b":"threading multiprocessing concurrent futures GIL shared state locking","n":"Choose concurrency by workload and synchronization needs rather than assuming threads make every program faster.","ex":"def execution_model(workload):\n    \"\"\"Choose a concurrency model based on the kind of work.\"\"\"\n    if workload == \"io\":\n        return \"threads\"\n    if workload == \"cpu\":\n        return \"processes\"\n    raise ValueError(\"workload must be io or cpu\")\n\nfor kind in (\"io\", \"cpu\"):\n    print(kind, \"->\", execution_model(kind))","lesson":"## I/O concurrency versus CPU parallelism\nThreads share one process and its memory. They are useful for overlapping blocking I/O, but the CPython Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) traditionally prevents multiple threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously in a single interpreter. Native extensions may release the GIL, and interpreter builds or future versions can differ, so benchmark the actual workload.\n```pylocal\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:\n    results = list(pool.map(fetch_one, urls))\n```\nFor CPU-bound pure Python work, multiple processes can use multiple cores at the cost of startup, serialization, and inter-process communication. Async I/O is another option when the libraries support it.\nCHECK: What is a common use for threads in standard CPython? ;; Overlapping blocking I/O operations ;; Automatically speeding up pure Python CPU loops ;; Eliminating the need for locks ;; Threads are often useful while tasks wait on I/O\n\n## Race conditions and process boundaries\nA race condition occurs when correctness depends on the timing of concurrent operations. The GIL does not make multi-step application invariants atomic; protect shared state with locks, queues, or message passing. Processes have separate memory by default, so sharing results requires explicit communication or shared-memory facilities.\n```pylocal\nfrom threading import Lock\nlock = Lock()\ncount = 0\ndef increment():\n    global count\n    with lock:\n        count += 1\n```\nPrefer minimizing shared mutable state over adding locks everywhere. For processes on cross-platform applications, keep the entry point guarded with `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` and make submitted functions and data serializable where required.\nCHECK: Does the GIL make every multi-step update thread-safe? ;; No, application invariants still need synchronization ;; Yes, all sequences are atomic ;; Only when using a list ;; The GIL is not a substitute for locks or message passing","q":[["Which model commonly overlaps many blocking I/O calls?",["Threads or async I/O","Only one CPU process","Recursion"],0,"Waiting can overlap even when CPU bytecode does not run in parallel."],["What should protect a shared multi-step invariant?",["A lock, queue, or other synchronization design","Hope that the GIL covers it","A print statement"],0,"Application-level atomicity must be designed explicitly."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `partition_work(n, workers)` returning contiguous half-open `(start, stop)` ranges that split `range(n)` as evenly as possible. Earlier ranges may receive one extra item. Reject negative n and non-positive workers; return an empty list when n is zero.","s":"def partition_work(n, workers):\n    pass\n","h":"Use `divmod(n, workers)` to find the base chunk size and remainder. Allocate one extra item to the first `remainder` chunks and skip empty chunks.","t":"assert partition_work(10,3)==[(0,4),(4,7),(7,10)],\"balanced contiguous chunks\"\nassert partition_work(5,8)==[(0,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5)],\"do not return empty ranges\"\nassert partition_work(0,3)==[],\"zero tasks return no ranges\"\nfor n,w in [(-1,2),(2,0)]:\n    try: partition_work(n,w)\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"validate n and workers\")"},"d":{"p":"This helper recommends threads for CPU-heavy Python work and processes for blocking I/O. Correct the mapping and reject unknown workload labels.","s":"def execution_model(workload):\n    if workload == \"cpu\":\n        return \"threads\"\n    if workload == \"io\":\n        return \"processes\"\n    raise ValueError(\"workload must be cpu or io\")\n","h":"Threads are often convenient for overlapping I/O; CPU-bound pure-Python work often benefits from processes when parallel CPU execution is needed.","t":"assert execution_model(\"cpu\")==\"processes\",\"CPU-heavy work can use multiple processes\"\nassert execution_model(\"io\")==\"threads\",\"threads can overlap blocking I/O\"\ntry:\n    execution_model(\"other\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"reject unknown workload\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Threads, Processes & the GIL","p":"Implement `expected_counter(workers, increments)`, a pure-Python reference calculation for checking the result of a concurrent counter. Each worker performs `increments` increments; reject negative or non-integer inputs. Explain why comparing a concurrent implementation against this reference is useful.","s":"def expected_counter(workers, increments):\n    pass\n","h":"Validate both inputs, then return the product. The point is a deterministic reference value; real concurrency and synchronization belong in a local threaded Python runtime.","t":"assert expected_counter(4,500)==2000,\"four workers with 500 increments each\"\nassert expected_counter(0,5)==0 and expected_counter(4,0)==0,\"zero work gives zero\"\nfor args in [(-1,3),(2,-1),(1.5,2),(2,3.0)]:\n    try: expected_counter(*args)\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"reject negative and non-integer arguments\")"}},{"t":"Asyncio: Cancellation & Backpressure","track":"Software Engineering","b":"asyncio TaskGroup Queue timeout cancellation semaphore","n":"Design asynchronous systems that bound resource usage and shut down predictably under failures and cancellation.","ex":"import asyncio\nasync def worker(queue):\n    while True:\n        item = await queue.get()\n        try:\n            if item is None: return\n            await asyncio.sleep(0)\n        finally:\n            queue.task_done()\n","lesson":"## Structured concurrency and cancellation\nA task should have a clear owner and lifetime. `asyncio.TaskGroup` (Python 3.11+) scopes child tasks to a block and waits for them before leaving; failures propagate in a structured way. Cancellation is delivered at an await point, so cleanup belongs in `try/finally` and cancellation should generally not be swallowed.\n```python\nasync def serve():\n    try:\n        await handle_requests()\n    finally:\n        await close_connections()\n```\nUse `asyncio.timeout()` where available or `wait_for` for bounded waits. Retries should be limited and used only when repeating the action is safe; cancelled requests may already have completed remotely.\nCHECK: Why should async cleanup use `finally`? ;; Cancellation and exceptions can interrupt the normal path ;; Async functions never raise exceptions ;; Finally blocks make code parallel ;; It ensures cleanup executes during unwinding\n\n## Backpressure, queues, and semaphores\nBackpressure prevents producers from creating work faster than consumers can handle it. `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` blocks producers when the queue is full, while a semaphore limits concurrent access to a resource such as a rate-limited API. Task creation should be bounded for large streams.\n```python\nqueue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=50)\nlimit = asyncio.Semaphore(8)\nasync def limited_call(item):\n    async with limit:\n        return await call_service(item)\n```\nA complete design specifies queue size, error reporting, retry budgets, task cancellation, and shutdown signals. Without these, a system can appear responsive in a demo but fail under load.\nCHECK: What does a bounded async queue provide? ;; Backpressure when work arrives faster than it can be consumed ;; Automatic retry of every failure ;; Unlimited buffering ;; Producers wait when the configured queue capacity is reached","q":[["What limits concurrent calls to an async service?",["An asyncio.Semaphore","An infinite queue","A type annotation"],0,"A semaphore lets only a configured number of tasks enter the section."],["When should cancellation generally be swallowed?",["Rarely; re-raise after required cleanup","Always","Only when the task has started"],0,"Callers need cancellation to remain observable."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `bounded_map(fn, items, limit)` for async function `fn`. Process inputs concurrently but never more than `limit` calls at once; return results in input order.","s":"import asyncio\nasync def bounded_map(fn, items, limit):\n    pass\n","h":"Use an `asyncio.Semaphore` and `asyncio.gather` over wrapper coroutines.","t":"import asyncio\nactive=0; peak=0\nasync def f(x):\n    global active,peak\n    active+=1;peak=max(peak,active)\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    active-=1;return x*2\nresult=await bounded_map(f,[1,2,3,4],2)\nassert result==[2,4,6,8] and peak<=2,\"respect the concurrency limit and preserve order\""},"d":{"p":"This queue helper only signals backpressure after the queue has already exceeded capacity. A full queue should apply backpressure when size equals capacity too.","s":"def should_block_producer(queue_size, capacity):\n    return queue_size > capacity\n","h":"The queue is full when its current size is equal to its configured capacity.","t":"assert should_block_producer(5,5) is True,\"a full queue blocks new work\"\nassert should_block_producer(4,5) is False,\"one free slot remains\"\nassert should_block_producer(0,0) is True,\"zero capacity cannot accept work\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Asyncio: Cancellation & Backpressure","p":"Write async `consume_all(queue, handler, workers)` that launches a fixed number of consumers, processes queue items until a sentinel `None`, calls `task_done()` exactly once per fetched item, and waits for queue completion.","s":"import asyncio\nasync def consume_all(queue, handler, workers):\n    pass\n","h":"Create worker coroutines; use `queue.get()`, `try/finally: queue.task_done()`, then gather the workers after inserting one sentinel per worker.","t":"import asyncio\nseen=[]\nasync def handler(x): seen.append(x)\nasync def test():\n    q=asyncio.Queue()\n    for x in range(6): await q.put(x)\n    await consume_all(q,handler,2)\n    assert sorted(seen)==list(range(6)),\"each queued item should be processed once\"\nawait test()"}},{"t":"Test Engineering & Property-Based Testing","track":"Software Engineering","b":"pytest fixtures parametrization property based testing Hypothesis invariants fuzzing","n":"Move beyond example-only testing: design contracts, properties, invariants, test doubles, and reproducible regression tests.","ex":"def normalize_pair(a, b):\n    return tuple(sorted((a, b)))\nfor x, y in [(1,2), (5,-1), (3,3)]:\n    assert normalize_pair(x,y) == normalize_pair(y,x)","lesson":"## Examples versus properties\nExample tests verify specific cases. Property-based tests verify general laws over many generated inputs: a sort is ordered and preserves the multiset, a serializer round-trips valid data, and a normalization function is idempotent. Hypothesis can generate edge cases and shrink failures to smaller counterexamples.\n```python\n# Property idea: normalize(normalize(x)) == normalize(x)\n# Property idea: decode(encode(value)) == value\n```\nA property must be true for every input in its stated domain. State preconditions clearly; a bad property can reject valid implementations or accidentally test only your assumptions.\nCHECK: Which is a useful property of an idempotent normalizer? ;; Applying it twice has the same result as applying it once ;; It always returns a list ;; It never raises on every possible object ;; Idempotence is a general behavioral law\n\n## Isolation, fixtures, and regression suites\nA test should control dependencies such as time, randomness, filesystem paths, network access, and environment variables. Fixtures arrange repeatable setup; parametrization applies one contract to many cases. Mocks are useful when interaction itself matters, but overly detailed call expectations create brittle tests.\n```python\n# Examples: empty input, one item, duplicates, invalid values,\n# extremely large input, and the failure mode promised by the API.\n```\nWhen a production bug is found, write a regression test before or alongside the fix. Keep the failing input small, deterministic, and specific enough that the test documents the defect.\nCHECK: Does high code coverage guarantee the assertions are meaningful? ;; No, coverage only shows that code ran ;; Yes, every bug must be covered ;; Coverage automatically proves correctness ;; Execution coverage is not the same as behavioral verification","q":[["What can property-based testing discover?",["Unexpected edge cases from generated inputs","Only syntax errors","Only the exact sample test cases"],0,"Generated cases explore a broader input space."],["What is a regression test?",["A test that preserves a previously fixed behavior","A benchmark only","A test that is removed after passing"],0,"Regression tests prevent known bugs from returning."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `is_sorted(values)` and `unique_sorted(values)`; test the properties that output is nondecreasing and contains exactly the distinct values from the input. Do not use `set` alone if it loses the sorted order.","s":"def is_sorted(values):\n    pass\ndef unique_sorted(values):\n    pass\n","h":"Compare adjacent pairs for order; sort a set of the input for unique_sorted.","t":"assert is_sorted([]) and is_sorted([1,1,2]) and not is_sorted([2,1]),\"sorted check handles boundaries\"\nassert unique_sorted([3,1,3,2])==[1,2,3],\"deduplicate and sort\""},"d":{"p":"is_nondecreasing rejects equal adjacent values. Fix the comparison so sorted sequences may contain duplicates.","s":"def is_nondecreasing(values):\n    return all(left < right for left, right in zip(values, values[1:]))\n","h":"Nondecreasing means each item is less than or equal to the next item, not strictly less.","t":"assert is_nondecreasing([1,1,2,4]) is True,\"equal neighbors are allowed\"\nassert is_nondecreasing([1,3,2]) is False,\"detect a descending pair\"\nassert is_nondecreasing([]) is True,\"empty sequence is sorted\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Test Engineering & Property-Based Testing","p":"Implement `run_cases(fn, cases)` where each case is `(args, expected)`; return a list of failure descriptions and do not stop after the first mismatch. If `fn` raises, record the exception type in the failure description.","s":"def run_cases(fn, cases):\n    pass\n","h":"Loop through cases, call `fn(*args)` inside try/except, and append a description only when behavior differs.","t":"assert run_cases(lambda x:x+1,[((1,),2),((2,),4)])==[\"case 2: expected 4, got 3\"],\"collect all mismatches with case numbers\"\ndef boom(x): raise ValueError(\"bad\")\nassert \"ValueError\" in run_cases(boom,[((0,),1)])[0],\"record exception type\""}},{"t":"Profiling & Performance Engineering","track":"Software Engineering","b":"timeit cProfile tracemalloc algorithmic bottlenecks benchmark variance","n":"Measure realistic workloads, identify the dominant cost, and optimize without changing behavior.","ex":"from timeit import timeit\ndata = list(range(10_000))\nprint(timeit(\"sum(data)\", globals=globals(), number=100))","lesson":"## Profile before optimizing\nPerformance work begins with a reproducible baseline and a realistic workload. `timeit` is good for small snippets; `cProfile` can show which functions account for time across a program; `tracemalloc` tracks many Python allocations. Measure several runs and focus on robust differences, not a single tiny timing.\n```python\nimport cProfile\ncProfile.run(\"sum(i*i for i in range(100_000))\")\n```\nDistinguish algorithmic cost from constant factors, I/O wait, network latency, and startup overhead. A microbenchmark can be misleading when its data shape or cache behavior differs from production.\nCHECK: Which tool is appropriate for a first function-level CPU profile? ;; `cProfile` ;; `copy.deepcopy` ;; `json.dumps` ;; Profiling aggregates execution costs by function\n\n## Complexity and memory tradeoffs\nOptimize the largest bottleneck first, then profile again. Replacing an O(n²) algorithm with O(n log n) often matters more than micro-optimizing a line. Memory optimizations can introduce recomputation, latency, or complexity; choose based on measured requirements.\n```python\n# A generator may reduce peak memory for one-pass work.\ntotal = sum(value * value for value in range(1_000_000))\n```\nPreserve correctness with regression tests and compare outputs before and after. For benchmarks, avoid logging in the timed region, control random inputs, warm up where relevant, and state the interpreter and machine used.\nCHECK: What is a good optimization loop? ;; Measure, change one bottleneck, then measure again ;; Rewrite everything at once ;; Guess which line is slow ;; Measurements prevent spending effort on non-dominant code","q":[["What does `cProfile` help identify?",["Which functions consume execution time","Which variable names are longest","Which functions have type hints"],0,"A profiler helps prioritize high-cost call paths."],["What should happen after an optimization?",["Measure again and verify correctness","Assume it is faster","Delete the tests"],0,"The effect must be observed, not presumed."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `chunked(iterable, size)` as a generator yielding lists of at most `size` elements, without materializing the entire input. Raise ValueError for non-positive sizes.","s":"def chunked(iterable, size):\n    pass\n","h":"Accumulate a small list and yield each full chunk, then yield the remainder.","t":"assert list(chunked(range(5),2))==[[0,1],[2,3],[4]],\"chunk into bounded lists\"\nassert list(chunked([],3))==[],\"empty input yields nothing\"\ntry: list(chunked([1],0))\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"size must be positive\")"},"d":{"p":"prefix_sums resets the running total on every iteration, so the results are not cumulative. Fix the accumulation without rescanning earlier values.","s":"def prefix_sums(values):\n    total = 0\n    result = []\n    for value in values:\n        total = value\n        result.append(total)\n    return result\n","h":"Update the running total by adding each new value, then append it. Each input should be processed once.","t":"assert prefix_sums([1,2,3,4])==[1,3,6,10],\"prefix totals should accumulate\"\nassert prefix_sums([])==[],\"empty input\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Profiling & Performance Engineering","p":"Implement `top_k_frequent(values, k)` using a collection counter and a deterministic tie-break: highest frequency first, then values ascending. Avoid sorting more than necessary if you can justify the choice.","s":"from collections import Counter\ndef top_k_frequent(values, k):\n    pass\n","h":"Sort `Counter(values).items()` using key `(-count, value)`; then slice carefully for k <= 0.","t":"assert top_k_frequent([1,1,2,2,3],2)==[1,2],\"ties break by ascending value\"\nassert top_k_frequent([],3)==[] and top_k_frequent([4],0)==[],\"empty and non-positive k\""}},{"t":"Packaging, Imports & Dependency Isolation","track":"Software Engineering","b":"venv pyproject.toml editable install wheels imports reproducibility","n":"Make Python projects reproducible and importable across machines, editors, and deployment environments.","ex":"# Reproducible project layout\n# pyproject.toml defines build metadata and dependencies\n# src/my_package/__init__.py contains the importable package\nimport sys\nprint(sys.executable)","lesson":"## Packages and environments\nA virtual environment isolates installed packages for one project. Activate it for convenience, or invoke its Python directly. Use `python -m pip` so pip is tied to the interpreter you intend to run. Avoid installing project dependencies into a system Python used by unrelated applications.\n```text\nproject/\n  pyproject.toml\n  src/my_package/__init__.py\n  tests/test_core.py\n```\nA package should have a deliberate public API. Keep tests, development tooling, and runtime dependencies distinguishable, and use a lock file or constraints strategy when exact reproducibility matters.\nCHECK: Why use `python -m pip` instead of a bare `pip` command? ;; It ties package installation to the selected Python interpreter ;; It installs packages into every environment ;; It creates a virtual environment automatically ;; It reduces interpreter mismatch problems\n\n## Imports and build metadata\nPython imports depend on `sys.path`, package layout, and installed metadata. Running a file directly from an unexpected directory can cause a different module with the same name to be imported. An installable package and a `pyproject.toml` make project behavior more consistent than depending on the current working directory.\n```toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=68\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n```\nFor deployment, pin or constrain critical dependencies, test in a clean environment, and build an artifact such as a wheel. Avoid relying on undeclared packages that happen to be installed on the developer laptop.\nCHECK: What should a clean-environment install test reveal? ;; Whether all required dependencies and package metadata are declared ;; Whether the developer has many global packages ;; Whether imports can always use the current directory ;; Clean installs expose undeclared dependencies","q":[["What does a virtual environment isolate?",["Project Python packages","Every file on the computer","The operating system kernel"],0,"A venv gives a project its own Python package environment."],["Why test a package from a clean install?",["To find missing declared dependencies","To make source code private","To eliminate all bugs"],0,"Hidden global installs can mask packaging errors."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `normalize_requirements(lines)` that strips whitespace, ignores blank/comment lines, lowercases package names before version specifiers, preserves version specifiers, and returns deterministic sorted output.","s":"def normalize_requirements(lines):\n    pass\n","h":"Strip each line, skip blank or leading-# lines, split at comparison operators, then sort normalized lines.","t":"assert normalize_requirements([\" Requests>=2 \",\"# note\",\"numpy==1.2\",\" \"])==[\"numpy==1.2\",\"requests>=2\"],\"normalize and sort requirements\""},"d":{"p":"Normalize a package-like name for use as a Python identifier. Hyphens and periods should become underscores, and names should be lowercase.","s":"def module_name(distribution):\n    return distribution\n","h":"Use lowercase and replace separator characters with underscores; avoid changing ordinary letters or digits.","t":"assert module_name(\"Fast-HTTP.Client\")==\"fast_http_client\",\"normalize common separators\"\nassert module_name(\"MyPackage2\")==\"mypackage2\",\"lowercase without losing digits\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Packaging, Imports & Dependency Isolation","p":"Implement `check_required(modules, available)` returning a sorted list of missing module names. Inputs are iterables; duplicate required names should appear only once.","s":"def check_required(modules, available):\n    pass\n","h":"Use sets for membership, then sort the difference.","t":"assert check_required([\"numpy\",\"torch\",\"numpy\"],[\"numpy\"])==[\"torch\"],\"return unique missing dependencies sorted\"\nassert check_required([],[])==[],\"no requirements means none are missing\""}},{"t":"Secure Python: Injection & Serialization","track":"Software Engineering","b":"input validation SQL injection shell injection pickle secrets path traversal","n":"Treat untrusted input as data, constrain capabilities, and use safe parsers and parameterized interfaces.","ex":"import json\npayload = '{\"mode\": \"safe\", \"count\": 3}'\nconfig = json.loads(payload)\nassert type(config[\"count\"]) is int\nprint(config)","lesson":"## Data is not executable code\nNever pass untrusted text to `eval`, `exec`, shell commands, or unsafe deserializers. JSON provides a data format for primitive structures; `pickle` can execute code during loading and must never be used on untrusted input. Validate the parsed shape and allowed values, not merely whether parsing succeeded.\n```python\nimport json\nobj = json.loads(payload)\nif not isinstance(obj, dict) or obj.get(\"mode\") not in {\"safe\", \"fast\"}:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid config\")\n```\nAvoid building SQL statements by concatenating user values. Use parameterized queries so the database treats values as data rather than SQL syntax.\nCHECK: Why must untrusted data not be loaded with `pickle.loads`? ;; Pickle deserialization can execute attacker-controlled behavior ;; Pickle only supports numbers ;; Pickle always loses data ;; The serialized object can trigger code during loading\n\n## Principle of least privilege\nValidate at trust boundaries, limit path and resource access, protect secrets from logs, and grant only the permissions a component needs. Escaping is context-specific: HTML, SQL, URLs, shell arguments, and JSON each have different rules. There is no universal function that safely quotes every context.\n```python\nimport subprocess\nsubprocess.run([\"tool\", \"--name\", user_value], check=True)\n```\nPassing an argument list avoids shell metacharacter interpretation, but the invoked program may still interpret the value as an option or filename. Use allowlists, option terminators where supported, size limits, and robust validation for the specific command.\nCHECK: Does escaping for HTML make a string safe for SQL? ;; No, escaping is context-specific ;; Yes, all contexts use the same rules ;; Only if the string is short ;; Every output context has its own syntax and defenses","q":[["Which is the preferred way to include user values in SQL?",["Parameterized queries","String concatenation","`eval` on the SQL"],0,"Bound parameters keep values separate from query syntax."],["Are escaping rules universal across output contexts?",["No, each context has its own syntax","Yes, HTML escaping works everywhere","Only paths need escaping"],0,"Use context-specific safe APIs."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `validate_config(obj)` for a dict requiring `mode` to be `safe` or `fast`, and `workers` to be an exact int from 1 to 32. Reject bool for workers and raise ValueError for malformed input.","s":"def validate_config(obj):\n    pass\n","h":"Check type(obj) is dict, allowed mode, and `type(workers) is int` before checking the range.","t":"assert validate_config({\"mode\":\"fast\",\"workers\":4})=={\"mode\":\"fast\",\"workers\":4},\"return valid configuration\"\nfor obj in ({\"mode\":\"fast\",\"workers\":True},{\"mode\":\"unsafe\",\"workers\":2},{\"mode\":\"safe\",\"workers\":0}):\n    try: validate_config(obj)\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"malformed config must be rejected\")"},"d":{"p":"This query builder interpolates user data directly into SQL text. Return a parameterized query and the user ID separately as a parameter tuple.","s":"def user_query(user_id):\n    sql = f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = {user_id}\"\n    return sql, ()\n","h":"Keep the SQL string fixed and return user-provided data as a separate bound parameter.","t":"sql,params=user_query(\"7 OR 1=1\")\nassert sql==\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?\",\"query must contain a placeholder\"\nassert params==(\"7 OR 1=1\",),\"user input must remain data, not SQL code\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Secure Python: Injection & Serialization","p":"Implement `safe_join(root, user_path)` which returns a resolved Path inside `root`, and raises ValueError if the resolved candidate escapes the root. Explain in a comment that filesystem race and symlink policies may require additional protection.","s":"from pathlib import Path\ndef safe_join(root, user_path):\n    pass\n","h":"Resolve both root and candidate, then require candidate to equal root or have root among its parents.","t":"base=Path(\"/tmp/app\")\nassert safe_join(base,\"files/a.txt\")==Path(\"/tmp/app/files/a.txt\"),\"valid relative path\"\ntry: safe_join(base,\"../../etc/passwd\")\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"path escape must be rejected\")"}},{"t":"HTTP Clients, Retries & Rate Limits","track":"Software Engineering","b":"HTTP status codes timeouts retry backoff idempotency Retry-After","n":"Build network clients that fail predictably, avoid retry storms, and respect remote service limits.","ex":"def retry_delay(attempt, base=0.5, cap=8.0):\n    return min(cap, base * (2 ** attempt))\nfor i in range(5):\n    print(retry_delay(i))","lesson":"## Timeouts and status classification\nEvery network request should have explicit connect/read or overall timeouts. A server can accept a connection but never return a response, so an unbounded wait can tie up resources indefinitely. Separate transport failures from HTTP status codes: a completed response with 404 is different from a connection reset.\n```python\n# With a library such as requests:\n# response = requests.get(url, timeout=(3.05, 10))\n# response.raise_for_status()\n```\nCheck the API contract before deciding which statuses are retryable. Most client errors are not fixed by repeating the same request, while transient server or throttling responses may be retryable under limits.\nCHECK: Why should an HTTP client set timeouts? ;; To prevent requests from waiting indefinitely ;; To guarantee the server responds successfully ;; To bypass rate limits ;; Timeouts bound resource use during stalled operations\n\n## Backoff, idempotency, and rate limits\nRetries amplify traffic during an outage. Use a maximum attempt count, exponential backoff, jitter, and any server-provided `Retry-After` guidance. Retry only when the operation is safe to repeat or an idempotency key prevents duplicate effects. A timeout after a POST does not prove the server did not process it.\n```python\ndef backoff(attempt, base=0.25, cap=10.0):\n    return min(cap, base * (2 ** attempt))\n```\nA rate limiter shapes outgoing traffic; a concurrency limit controls how many calls are in flight. These solve different problems. Record attempt counts and final failures, but avoid logging authorization headers or sensitive request bodies.\nCHECK: Why is retrying a timed-out payment POST dangerous without idempotency? ;; The server may have processed the first request already ;; POST requests cannot contain data ;; Every timeout means the server crashed ;; A repeated non-idempotent request can duplicate the side effect","q":[["What should a retry policy include?",["Maximum attempts and backoff","Unlimited immediate retries","No timeout"],0,"Bounded backoff prevents retry storms."],["What does an idempotency key help with?",["Preventing duplicate effects from retried requests","Encrypting the connection","Making every status code success"],0,"The server can recognize repeated attempts for one logical operation."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `retryable(status, method, attempt, max_attempts)` that permits retries only before the max attempt, for statuses 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, and for GET/HEAD only unless a write method is explicitly idempotent in your policy. Use an allowlisted method set.","s":"def retryable(status, method, attempt, max_attempts=4):\n    pass\n","h":"Normalize method with upper(); first reject exhausted attempts, then require an allowed status and safe method.","t":"assert retryable(503,\"GET\",1) and retryable(429,\"HEAD\",2),\"transient safe requests can retry\"\nassert not retryable(503,\"POST\",1) and not retryable(404,\"GET\",1) and not retryable(503,\"GET\",4),\"unsafe, permanent, or exhausted requests should not retry\""},"d":{"p":"retry_delay has an off-by-one exponent: its first retry waits twice the base delay. Use base * 2**attempt, capped at cap, and reject negative attempts.","s":"def retry_delay(attempt, base=1.0, cap=10.0):\n    if attempt < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"attempt must be non-negative\")\n    return min(cap, base * (2 ** (attempt + 1)))\n","h":"Attempt zero is the first retry delay, so its multiplier should be 2**0. Always cap exponential growth.","t":"assert retry_delay(0)==1.0,\"first backoff uses base delay\"\nassert retry_delay(2)==4.0,\"third retry doubles twice\"\nassert retry_delay(10,base=1,cap=5)==5,\"respect maximum delay\"\ntry:\n    retry_delay(-1)\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"negative attempts are invalid\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: HTTP Clients, Retries & Rate Limits","p":"Implement `parse_retry_after(value, now)` supporting either integer seconds or an HTTP-date string; return a non-negative delay in seconds or None for invalid input. Keep parsing deterministic by accepting `now` explicitly.","s":"from datetime import datetime, timezone\ndef parse_retry_after(value, now):\n    pass\n","h":"Try integer seconds first; otherwise parse an HTTP date, normalize both datetimes to UTC, and clamp the difference at zero.","t":"from datetime import datetime, timezone\nnow=datetime(2026,1,1,tzinfo=timezone.utc)\nassert parse_retry_after(\"12\",now)==12,\"integer delay\"\nassert parse_retry_after(\"bad\",now) is None,\"invalid value\""}},{"t":"SQL Transactions & Data Pipelines","track":"Software Engineering","b":"sqlite3 parameterized queries transactions ACID ETL batch idempotency","n":"Model data ingestion as a pipeline with clear validation, transactional writes, and repeatable outcomes.","ex":"import sqlite3\ncon = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ncon.execute(\"create table item (id integer primary key, name text)\")\ncon.execute(\"insert into item(name) values (?)\", (\"widget\",))\nprint(con.execute(\"select id, name from item\").fetchall())\ncon.close()","lesson":"## Queries and transactions\nUse parameterized queries, never concatenate user-provided values into SQL. A transaction groups updates so either the logical operation succeeds or it can be rolled back. ACID describes atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability; real guarantees depend on the database and configuration.\n```python\ncon.execute(\"INSERT INTO item(name) VALUES (?)\", (name,))\n```\nKeep transactions short and ensure failures cause rollback when needed. Do not hold a database transaction open while waiting on a slow HTTP request unless the design specifically requires it.\nCHECK: What does a transaction provide for a group of related writes? ;; A unit that can commit or roll back together ;; Automatic encryption of every field ;; A guarantee that all queries are fast ;; Transactions protect consistency across related changes\n\n## ETL, idempotency, and validation\nETL means extract, transform, load. A good ingestion pipeline validates inputs, normalizes schema, deduplicates records, batches writes, and records failures so the operation can resume. Idempotent stages can be rerun without duplicating effects, often using stable keys or upserts.\n```python\n# Parse -> validate -> normalize -> deduplicate -> write in a transaction\n```\nUse explicit schema migration strategy and treat malformed records deliberately: reject, quarantine, or fix them according to a policy. Silent coercion can corrupt data while making the pipeline appear successful.\nCHECK: Why is idempotency useful in a data pipeline? ;; Rerunning a stage does not duplicate its intended effect ;; It makes malformed input valid ;; It removes the need for transactions ;; Idempotency supports safe retries and recovery","q":[["How should user values enter SQL statements?",["Through bound parameters","By concatenating raw strings","By calling eval"],0,"Parameters preserve the separation between statement and data."],["What is a useful ETL property?",["Stages are validated and safely repeatable","Every row is silently coerced","Errors are discarded"],0,"Repeatability and clear error policy support reliable recovery."]],"x":{"p":"Implement `normalize_rows(rows)` where each row is a dict with id and name. Trim names, convert id to int, reject blank names, and keep the first valid row per id while preserving input order.","s":"def normalize_rows(rows):\n    pass\n","h":"Use a `seen` set for IDs and build an output list only after a row passes validation.","t":"rows=[{\"id\":\"1\",\"name\":\" Ada \"},{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"duplicate\"},{\"id\":2,\"name\":\"Grace\"}]\nassert normalize_rows(rows)==[(1,\"Ada\"),(2,\"Grace\")],\"normalize and deduplicate by ID\""},"d":{"p":"aggregate_totals should sum amounts by category, but this version overwrites each earlier amount when a category appears again. Accumulate the values.","s":"def aggregate_totals(rows):\n    totals = {}\n    for category, amount in rows:\n        totals[category] = amount\n    return totals\n","h":"For each row, add the amount to the current total for that category, starting from zero.","t":"assert aggregate_totals([(\"a\",2),(\"b\",7),(\"a\",5)])=={\"a\":7,\"b\":7},\"sum repeated categories\"\nassert aggregate_totals([])=={},\"empty input\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: SQL Transactions & Data Pipelines","p":"Implement `apply_batch(store, events)` that applies `(key, delta)` events atomically: if any delta is non-integer or negative, leave the original dict unchanged and raise ValueError; otherwise return a new dict with the sums.","s":"def apply_batch(store, events):\n    pass\n","h":"Validate all events before changing a copy, or mutate only a copy and return it after all checks pass.","t":"base={\"a\":2};assert apply_batch(base,[(\"a\",3),(\"b\",1)])=={\"a\":5,\"b\":1},\"apply the full batch\"\ntry: apply_batch(base,[(\"a\",1),(\"b\",-1)])\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"invalid batch must fail\")\nassert base=={\"a\":2},\"original state must be unchanged after failure\""}},{"t":"Numerical Computing with NumPy","track":"Data & Scientific Computing","b":"numpy ndarray vectorization broadcasting axis dtype memory views","n":"Learn array-based numerical computing: shapes, broadcasting, vectorization, reductions, and memory layout.","ex":"# This is NumPy code for a real NumPy environment, not the browser runner.\nimport numpy as np\nx = np.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], dtype=np.float32)\nprint(x.shape, x.mean(axis=0))","lesson":"## Arrays, shapes, and dtypes\nA NumPy `ndarray` stores a typed, usually homogeneous block of numeric data with a shape and strides. Unlike a Python list of separate objects, array elements use a fixed dtype, making vectorized operations efficient. Shape describes the dimensions; dtype describes the element representation; strides describe how indices map to memory.\n```numpy\nimport numpy as np\nx = np.arange(12, dtype=np.float32).reshape(3, 4)\nprint(x.shape, x.dtype, x.ndim)\n```\nKeep dimensions explicit. A vector of shape `(4,)` is not identical to a column matrix `(4, 1)`. Many numerical bugs are shape bugs that can be caught with assertions close to the data boundary.\nCHECK: What does `x.shape` describe for a NumPy array? ;; The length of each dimension ;; The data type only ;; The array maximum ;; Shape records the array dimensions\n\n## Broadcasting and vectorization\nBroadcasting allows elementwise operations across compatible shapes by comparing dimensions from the right: dimensions must match or one of them must be 1. For example `(3, 1)` and `(1, 4)` broadcast to `(3, 4)`. Broadcasting does not generally copy the small input into a large physical array; it defines how the operation views it.\n```numpy\nx = np.arange(3).reshape(3, 1)\ny = np.arange(4).reshape(1, 4)\nprint((x + y).shape)  # (3, 4)\n```\nVectorization moves loops into optimized array operations, but it can create large intermediates. Use axis reductions intentionally and profile memory for large arrays. Check dtype promotion when mixing integers, floats, and lower-precision values.\nCHECK: What shape results from broadcasting `(3, 1)` with `(1, 4)`? ;; `(3, 4)` ;; `(3, 1, 4)` ;; `(1, 1)` ;; Each of the mismatched dimensions expands to their compatible maximum","q":[["What does broadcasting compare first?",["Dimensions from the right","Only the first dimension","Every value element by element"],0,"Broadcast compatibility is checked from trailing dimensions."],["Why specify a dtype?",["To control numeric representation and memory behavior","To sort an array","To make every operation exact"],0,"dtype controls what kind of values the array stores."]],"x":{"p":"Implement pure-Python helpers `broadcast_dim(a, b)` and `broadcast_shape(left, right)` that compute the NumPy-style broadcast result or raise ValueError for incompatible dimensions. Dimensions are positive integers or 1; allow leading implicit dimensions of size 1.","s":"def broadcast_dim(a, b):\n    pass\ndef broadcast_shape(left, right):\n    pass\n","h":"Right-align the shapes. Each pair is valid if equal or one value is 1; the result dimension is the maximum.","t":"assert broadcast_shape((3,1),(1,4))==(3,4),\"broadcast result\"\nassert broadcast_shape((5,3,4,1),(3,1,7))==(5,3,4,7),\"left-pad shapes with ones\"\ntry: broadcast_shape((2,),(3,))\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"incompatible dimensions must fail\")"},"d":{"p":"Implement NumPy-style shape broadcasting without importing NumPy. Dimensions are compatible when they match or one of them is 1; compare from the right.","s":"def broadcast_shapes(a, b):\n    result = []\n    for left, right in zip(a, b):\n        if left != right:\n            raise ValueError(\"incompatible shapes\")\n        result.append(left)\n    return tuple(result)\n","h":"Right-align shapes, treating missing leading dimensions as 1. For each pair choose the non-1 dimension, or fail if neither is 1 and they differ.","t":"assert broadcast_shapes((3,1),(1,4))==(3,4),\"broadcast singleton dimensions\"\nassert broadcast_shapes((5,3,1),(3,4))==(5,3,4),\"align from the right\"\ntry:\n    broadcast_shapes((2,3),(4,3))\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"incompatible dimensions should fail\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: Numerical Computing with NumPy","p":"Implement `reduce_shape(shape, axis, keepdims=False)` that returns the output shape after summing along one axis. Support negative axes and raise ValueError for invalid axes.","s":"def reduce_shape(shape, axis, keepdims=False):\n    pass\n","h":"Normalize a negative axis with `axis += len(shape)`. Either remove that dimension or replace it with 1.","t":"assert reduce_shape((2,3,4),1)==(2,4),\"reduction removes the axis\"\nassert reduce_shape((2,3,4),-1,True)==(2,3,1),\"negative axis and keepdims\""}},{"t":"PyTorch 1: Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","track":"PyTorch & Deep Learning","b":"torch.tensor Tensor shape dtype device broadcasting view reshape","n":"Learn the tensor model, shape arithmetic, broadcasting, device placement, and the CPU/GPU boundary.","ex":"# Run in Google Colab or a local environment with PyTorch installed.\nimport torch\nx = torch.tensor([[1., 2.], [3., 4.]])\ny = torch.tensor([10., 20.])\nprint(x.shape, (x + y).shape)\nprint(x.device, x.dtype)","lesson":"## Tensor mental model\nA PyTorch tensor is a multidimensional typed array with shape, dtype, device, and layout. Most model inputs are tensors with batch and feature dimensions; image batches are often `(N, C, H, W)`. Shape errors usually mean a dimension was omitted, swapped, or interpreted differently than intended.\n```pytorch\nimport torch\nx = torch.randn(32, 3, 224, 224)  # N, C, H, W\nprint(x.shape, x.dtype, x.device)\n```\nCreate tensors with an explicit dtype when precision matters. Integer labels for class-index losses are commonly `torch.long`; neural network weights usually use floating dtypes. Avoid creating a tensor from nested Python lists in a hot loop when data can be batched efficiently.\nCHECK: What do the dimensions in `(32, 3, 224, 224)` commonly mean for image input? ;; Batch, channels, height, width ;; Height, width, batch, labels ;; One 32-channel image ;; The common convention is NCHW image batches\n\n## Broadcasting and device movement\nBroadcasting compares dimensions from the right. Matching dimensions or a size of 1 are compatible; incompatible dimensions raise a runtime error. A `(batch, features)` tensor and a `(features,)` bias usually combine as intended. Be deliberate about adding singleton dimensions with `unsqueeze` and avoid accidental broadcasting that produces a much larger tensor than expected.\n```pytorch\nbias = torch.zeros(64)\nactivations = torch.randn(16, 64)\nresult = activations + bias  # (16, 64)\n```\nTensors and models must be on compatible devices. Move both model and data to the same selected device, and do not assume CUDA exists. `.view()` generally requires compatible strides; `.reshape()` may copy when needed. Check shapes after transformations while learning.\nCHECK: If shapes are `(16, 64)` and `(64,)`, what is the result shape after addition? ;; `(16, 64)` ;; `(16, 128)` ;; `(64, 16)` ;; The one-dimensional bias broadcasts across the batch dimension","q":[["Which attribute reports a tensor's dimensions?",["shape","device","grad_fn only"],0,"Shape communicates tensor dimensionality."],["What must usually match for a tensor operation?",["Compatible shapes and devices","Only variable names","Only the batch size"],0,"Shape and device compatibility are checked by PyTorch."]],"x":{"p":"Implement pure-Python `broadcast_shape(a, b)` using PyTorch/NumPy broadcast rules: compare right-aligned dimensions, permit equal values or 1, and raise ValueError for incompatibility.","s":"def broadcast_shape(a, b):\n    pass\n","h":"Pad the shorter shape on the left with 1s. The output dimension is the larger compatible dimension.","t":"assert broadcast_shape((8,1,32),(1,4,32))==(8,4,32),\"broadcast leading and middle dimensions\"\nassert broadcast_shape((64,),(16,64))==(16,64),\"feature vector broadcasts over batch\""},"d":{"p":"A linear layer maps each input row to out_features values. Fix this shape helper so it preserves the leading batch dimension.","s":"def linear_output_shape(input_shape, out_features):\n    return (out_features,)\n","h":"A linear layer transforms the final feature dimension; batch and other leading dimensions stay intact.","t":"assert linear_output_shape((8,12),4)==(8,4),\"preserve batch dimension\"\nassert linear_output_shape((2,5,12),7)==(2,5,7),\"preserve all leading dimensions\"\ntry:\n    linear_output_shape((12,),0)\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"out_features must be positive\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: PyTorch 1: Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","p":"Create a pure-Python `image_batch_shape(batch, channels, height, width)` validator that returns the NCHW shape and rejects any non-positive or non-integer dimensions. This models the checks you should do before constructing an image tensor.","s":"def image_batch_shape(batch, channels, height, width):\n    pass\n","h":"Validate type with `type(value) is int` so bool is rejected, then require every dimension > 0.","t":"assert image_batch_shape(32,3,224,224)==(32,3,224,224),\"NCHW shape\"\nfor bad in ((0,3,224,224),(True,3,224,224),(1,0,2,2)):\n    try: image_batch_shape(*bad)\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"invalid dimensions must fail\")"}},{"t":"PyTorch 2: Autograd & Gradient Debugging","track":"PyTorch & Deep Learning","b":"torch.autograd requires_grad backward grad detach no_grad gradient accumulation","n":"Understand how PyTorch builds a computation graph, accumulates gradients, and helps diagnose learning failures.","ex":"# Run in a PyTorch environment.\nimport torch\nw = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\nloss = (w - 5) ** 2\nloss.backward()\nprint(loss.item(), w.grad.item())  # 4.0, -4.0","lesson":"## The computation graph\nAutograd records tensor operations involving tensors that require gradients. Calling `loss.backward()` computes derivatives and stores gradients in leaf tensors such as trainable parameters. Gradients accumulate by default, so training loops normally clear them before the next backward pass.\n```pytorch\nw = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\nloss = (w - 5) ** 2\nloss.backward()\nprint(w.grad)  # derivative: 2 * (w - 5)\n```\nThe derivative tells how the loss changes with a small parameter change. It is not the updated weight; the optimizer applies a parameter update separately. A missing gradient can signal that a tensor was detached, the path did not contribute to the loss, or the graph was not built.\nCHECK: What does `loss.backward()` do for a scalar loss? ;; Computes gradients through the recorded graph ;; Automatically updates every parameter ;; Clears gradients before computing ;; Backward computes derivatives; optimizers perform updates\n\n## Gradient accumulation and debugging\nGradients accumulate into `.grad`, which is useful for gradient accumulation across mini-batches but surprising if unintended. Standard loops call `optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)`, compute predictions and loss, call `backward()`, then `optimizer.step()`. Use `torch.no_grad()` or `torch.inference_mode()` for evaluation, not for the training forward pass that needs gradients.\n```pytorch\noptimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\nprediction = model(inputs)\nloss = loss_fn(prediction, targets)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\n```\nInspect for `None`, NaN, or exploding gradients. Gradient clipping can help some unstable recurrent or deep models, but it should not hide a wrong loss scale, bad normalization, or incorrect tensor shapes. Use `detach()` only when intentionally stopping gradient flow.\nCHECK: Why clear gradients before an ordinary training iteration? ;; PyTorch accumulates gradients by default ;; Backward clears old gradients automatically ;; Clearing gradients updates weights ;; Clearing avoids mixing the current gradient with previous batches unintentionally","q":[["Where is the gradient of a leaf parameter commonly stored?",["In its `.grad` attribute","In `.shape`","In `.device`"],0,"Autograd stores accumulated gradients on leaf tensors."],["Does `optimizer.step()` compute the backward pass?",["No, it updates parameters using gradients already computed","Yes, it always calls backward","It only clears gradients"],0,"Backward computes derivatives; step applies the optimizer update."]],"x":{"p":"Implement the pure-Python derivative `quadratic_grad(w, target)` for loss `(w - target) ** 2`, returning `2 * (w - target)`. This checks the calculus behind autograd without requiring torch in the browser runner.","s":"def quadratic_grad(w, target):\n    pass\n","h":"Differentiate the square: the derivative of `(w - target)^2` with respect to w is twice the difference.","t":"assert quadratic_grad(3,5)==-4 and quadratic_grad(5,5)==0,\"quadratic gradient\"\nassert quadratic_grad(-2,1)==-6,\"negative parameters are valid\""},"d":{"p":"Compute the number of gradient-accumulation microbatches required for a total batch size. Reject values that are not positive or that do not divide evenly.","s":"def accumulation_steps(total_batch, micro_batch):\n    if total_batch < 0 or micro_batch < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"batch sizes must be positive\")\n    return total_batch // micro_batch\n","h":"Batch sizes must be positive and the total batch must be an exact multiple of the micro-batch size.","t":"assert accumulation_steps(64,8)==8,\"eight microbatches make total batch 64\"\nassert accumulation_steps(12,3)==4,\"four microbatches\"\nfor a,b in [(0,2),(8,0),(10,3)]:\n    try: accumulation_steps(a,b)\n    except ValueError: pass\n    else: raise AssertionError(\"invalid or non-divisible batch sizes must fail\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: PyTorch 2: Autograd & Gradient Debugging","p":"Implement `gradient_descent_step(w, target, lr)` for the scalar quadratic loss `(w - target)^2`: return the updated weight `w - lr * gradient`. Reject non-positive learning rates.","s":"def gradient_descent_step(w, target, lr):\n    pass\n","h":"Compute the gradient first, then subtract learning rate times gradient.","t":"assert gradient_descent_step(3,5,0.1)==3.4,\"the negative gradient moves w toward 5\"\nassert gradient_descent_step(5,5,0.1)==5,\"zero gradient means no update\"\ntry: gradient_descent_step(1,2,0)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"learning rate must be positive\")"}},{"t":"PyTorch 3: nn.Module, Losses & Optimizers","track":"PyTorch & Deep Learning","b":"torch.nn Module forward cross entropy optimizer state_dict logits","n":"Build models with modules, select a loss that matches the task, and write a correct optimization step.","ex":"# Run in a PyTorch environment.\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\nmodel = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(4, 16), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(16, 3))\nx = torch.randn(8, 4)\nlogits = model(x)\nprint(logits.shape)  # (8, 3)","lesson":"## Models, modules, and logits\nSubclass `torch.nn.Module` for reusable architectures; define trainable layers in `__init__` and describe the forward computation in `forward`. Calling `model(x)` invokes the module machinery and hooks, so use it instead of calling `model.forward(x)` directly. A classifier with three classes commonly outputs logits shaped `(batch, 3)` before softmax.\n```pytorch\nfrom torch import nn\nclass Classifier(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, features, classes):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(features, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, classes))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)\n```\nA module registers parameters and buffers so `.to(device)`, `state_dict()`, and optimizers can discover them. Store layers as attributes or in `ModuleList`/`Sequential`, not an ordinary list that hides their parameters from module registration.\nCHECK: Why call `super().__init__()` in a custom `nn.Module`? ;; It initializes PyTorch module registration behavior ;; It trains the model automatically ;; It chooses the loss function ;; Parameters and child modules need correct registration\n\n## Losses, optimizers, and a training step\nChoose the loss based on target semantics. `CrossEntropyLoss` expects raw class logits and integer class indices; it internally combines log-softmax and negative log likelihood. `BCEWithLogitsLoss` is commonly used for binary or multi-label classification with raw logits. Do not apply softmax before `CrossEntropyLoss` unless a separate use specifically needs probabilities.\n```pytorch\noptimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\nlogits = model(inputs)\nloss = loss_fn(logits, targets)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\n```\nTrack loss values, learning rate, and validation metrics. Save `model.state_dict()` for model weights and recreate the architecture before loading it; full training resumption also needs optimizer and scheduler state plus relevant metadata.\nCHECK: What should be passed to `CrossEntropyLoss` for classification? ;; Raw logits and integer class-index targets ;; Softmax probabilities and one-hot strings only ;; Class names as text ;; The loss expects logits and class indices for this common usage","q":[["What does the final dimension of `(batch, 3)` usually represent here?",["Three class logits","The number of training epochs","The learning rate"],0,"Each example receives one score per class."],["What is the correct main training order?",["zero_grad, forward, loss, backward, step","step, forward, zero_grad, loss","backward before computing loss"],0,"The optimizer step uses gradients from the current backward pass."]],"x":{"p":"Implement pure-Python `linear_output_shape(batch, in_features, out_features)` that validates positive integer dimensions and returns `(batch, out_features)`. This models the shape contract of `nn.Linear`.","s":"def linear_output_shape(batch, in_features, out_features):\n    pass\n","h":"The last input dimension must match in_features; the output retains leading dimensions and replaces the final feature size.","t":"assert linear_output_shape(32,128,10)==(32,10),\"linear layer changes the final feature dimension\"\ntry: linear_output_shape(0,3,2)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"batch size must be positive\")"},"d":{"p":"classification_accuracy receives rows of class logits and the expected class IDs. Fix it to compare each row’s argmax with its matching target.","s":"def classification_accuracy(logits, targets):\n    if not logits:\n        return 0.0\n    predicted = [max(row) for row in logits]\n    return sum(p == t for p, t in zip(predicted, targets)) / len(targets)\n","h":"max(row) returns the largest score, not its class index. Use the index of the largest score and validate matching batch sizes.","t":"assert classification_accuracy([[0.1,0.9],[0.8,0.2]],[1,0])==1.0,\"both predictions are correct\"\nassert classification_accuracy([[3,1],[0,4]],[0,0])==0.5,\"one of two predictions is correct\"\nassert classification_accuracy([],[])==0.0,\"empty input returns zero\""},"p":{"n":"Applied project: PyTorch 3: nn.Module, Losses & Optimizers","p":"Implement `cross_entropy_row(logits, target)` for a single row using stable log-sum-exp; logits is a list of real scores and target is a valid class index. Return the scalar negative log-probability for that class.","s":"import math\ndef cross_entropy_row(logits, target):\n    pass\n","h":"Subtract max(logits) before exponentiating. loss = log(sum(exp(score - max))) + max - target_logit.","t":"assert abs(cross_entropy_row([0.0,0.0],0)-math.log(2))<1e-9,\"uniform two-class loss\"\nassert abs(cross_entropy_row([10.0,0.0],0)-0.0000454)<1e-4,\"confident correct class has small loss\""}},{"t":"PyTorch 4: DataLoaders, Validation & Overfitting","track":"PyTorch & Deep Learning","b":"Dataset DataLoader train eval inference_mode validation leakage early stopping","n":"Move from a model that runs to an experiment that measures generalization honestly.","ex":"# Run in a PyTorch environment.\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\nimport torch\nx = torch.randn(100, 4)\ny = (x[:, 0] > 0).long()\nloader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(x, y), batch_size=16, shuffle=True)\nfor batch_x, batch_y in loader:\n    print(batch_x.shape, batch_y.shape)\n    break","lesson":"## Data pipelines and batching\nA `Dataset` defines how one example is retrieved; a `DataLoader` handles batching, optional shuffling, worker processes, and collation. Keep input and target shapes consistent with the model and loss. Shuffle training data when order should not convey structure, but do not randomly reshuffle the validation set for each reported comparison unless there is a specific reason.\n```pytorch\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\ntrain_ds = TensorDataset(train_x, train_y)\ntrain_loader = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=64, shuffle=True)\n```\nFor large datasets, avoid loading everything into Python lists if the dataset can be streamed or indexed efficiently. When using multiple workers, account for platform start methods and keep dataset worker functions picklable where required.\nCHECK: What is the main role of a `DataLoader`? ;; Batch and iterate through dataset examples ;; Define the neural network layers ;; Compute the optimizer gradient ;; It combines a dataset with batching and iteration behavior\n\n## Validation, overfitting, and experiment hygiene\nTraining loss indicates fit to the training data; validation performance estimates generalization on held-out examples. Overfitting often appears when training loss improves while validation performance stalls or worsens. Keep the test set untouched until model choices are complete, because repeated test-set tuning leaks information into development decisions.\n```pytorch\nmodel.eval()\nwith torch.inference_mode():\n    logits = model(validation_inputs)\n```\n`model.eval()` switches behavior for modules such as dropout and batch normalization; inference mode disables autograd overhead. These do different jobs. Also call `model.train()` before the next training phase. Seed randomness when useful, but understand that determinism can vary by hardware and kernels.\nCHECK: Why use both `model.eval()` and `torch.inference_mode()` during evaluation? ;; One changes module behavior; the other disables autograd tracking ;; They are identical operations ;; One moves tensors to the GPU ;; Evaluation needs both behavior mode and gradient-mode control","q":[["Which dataset should guide early stopping and hyperparameter selection?",["Validation set","Final test set repeatedly","Training labels only"],0,"Keep the test set independent of iterative model choice."],["What does `model.eval()` control?",["Module-specific training/evaluation behavior","Gradient computation by itself","Tensor dtype"],0,"Evaluation mode affects modules such as Dropout and BatchNorm."]],"x":{"p":"Implement pure-Python `split_indices(n, validation_fraction)` returning `(train_indices, validation_indices)`. Use a deterministic final slice for validation, validate parameters, and ensure every index appears exactly once.","s":"def split_indices(n, validation_fraction):\n    pass\n","h":"Compute a validation count, then split `range(n)` at `n - validation_count`.","t":"train,val=split_indices(10,0.2)\nassert train==list(range(8)) and val==[8,9],\"deterministic holdout split\"\nassert len(set(train+val))==10,\"no index is lost or duplicated\""},"d":{"p":"Build a deterministic train/validation split without leaking the same sample into both sets. Keep at least one validation item when n >= 2 and validate the ratio.","s":"def split_indices(n, val_ratio=0.2):\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non-negative\")\n    cut = int(n * (1 - val_ratio))\n    indices = list(range(n))\n    return indices[:cut], indices[cut:]\n","h":"Validate that 0 < val_ratio < 1. For n >= 2, clamp the validation count to 1..n-1; then slice one index range.","t":"train,val=split_indices(10,0.2)\nassert len(train)==8 and len(val)==2,\"split at requested ratio\"\nassert set(train).isdisjoint(val) and sorted(train+val)==list(range(10)),\"no overlap or missing indices\"\ntrain,val=split_indices(2,0.01)\nassert len(val)==1 and len(train)==1,\"retain train and validation for n>=2\"\ntry: split_indices(5,1.0)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"invalid validation ratio\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: PyTorch 4: DataLoaders, Validation & Overfitting","p":"Implement `classification_metrics(predictions, targets, classes)` returning accuracy and a confusion matrix with true labels on rows and predicted labels on columns. Validate equal lengths and label ranges.","s":"def classification_metrics(predictions, targets, classes):\n    pass\n","h":"Initialize a classes-by-classes zero matrix. For each pair, increment matrix[true][pred].","t":"acc,cm=classification_metrics([0,1,1],[0,0,1],2)\nassert abs(acc-2/3)<1e-9 and cm==[[1,1],[0,1]],\"accuracy and confusion matrix convention\""}},{"t":"PyTorch 5: CNNs, Transfer Learning & Reproducibility","track":"PyTorch & Deep Learning","b":"Conv2d pooling receptive field pretrained weights freeze parameters transform seeds checkpoint","n":"Finish with image models, transfer learning, and a reproducible experiment workflow.","ex":"# Run in Google Colab or a local PyTorch environment.\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\nmodel = nn.Sequential(\n    nn.Conv2d(3, 16, kernel_size=3, padding=1), nn.ReLU(),\n    nn.MaxPool2d(2), nn.Conv2d(16, 32, 3, padding=1), nn.ReLU(),\n    nn.AdaptiveAvgPool2d((1, 1)), nn.Flatten(), nn.Linear(32, 10)\n)\nprint(model(torch.randn(4, 3, 64, 64)).shape)  # (4, 10)","lesson":"## CNN shape arithmetic\nA convolution learns local filters shared across image positions. For one spatial dimension, the output size is `floor((input + 2*padding - dilation*(kernel - 1) - 1) / stride + 1)`. Pooling reduces spatial resolution; channels represent learned feature maps. Track height and width at each stage rather than guessing from a diagram.\n```pytorch\nnn.Conv2d(in_channels=3, out_channels=32, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1)\n```\nThe common image tensor layout is `(batch, channels, height, width)`. Normalize inputs consistently with the model or pretrained weights. A normalization mismatch can damage performance even when every tensor shape is correct.\nCHECK: What does `out_channels` specify for `Conv2d`? ;; The number of learned output feature maps ;; The batch size ;; The input image height ;; Each output channel corresponds to a learned filter bank\n\n## Transfer learning and freezing layers\nTransfer learning starts with features learned on a larger dataset. Load pretrained weights, apply the expected image preprocessing, replace the final classification layer, and train the new head first. Freezing parameters with `requires_grad_(False)` avoids computing gradients for them; if later fine-tuning, unfreeze selected blocks with a suitably smaller learning rate.\n```pytorch\nfor param in model.parameters():\n    param.requires_grad = False\n# Replace the final classifier with a new layer matching your class count.\n# Then pass only trainable parameters to the optimizer.\n```\nUse weights and preprocessing from a documented source. Do not assume every pretrained model expects the same image size or channel normalization. Train/validation splitting must also avoid near-duplicate images crossing the split.\nCHECK: Why must preprocessing match a pretrained model’s expected transforms? ;; Its learned features assume a compatible input distribution ;; Preprocessing changes the model class name ;; It guarantees zero validation error ;; Incorrect normalization can shift inputs away from the training distribution\n\n## Reproducible checkpoints and final audit\nA reproducible run records configuration, dataset version and split, random seeds, library/hardware details, metrics, and checkpoints. Save a state dictionary, optimizer state when resuming, and metadata such as class names. Set seeds for Python, NumPy, and PyTorch when used; exact determinism can require additional backend flags and may reduce performance.\n```pytorch\ntorch.save({\"model\": model.state_dict(), \"classes\": class_names}, \"checkpoint.pt\")\n# Recreate the same architecture before loading the state dictionary.\n```\nBefore trusting a result, compare with a simple baseline, inspect class imbalance, examine errors by class, and verify there is no train/validation leakage. Strong engineering is part of machine learning, not a final cosmetic step.\nCHECK: What should a useful model checkpoint include beyond weights when needed? ;; Metadata such as class names and experiment configuration ;; Only a screenshot of the training curve ;; The entire test set in the model file ;; Metadata makes model reuse and interpretation safer","q":[["What is the common shape for a batch of RGB images?",["(N, 3, H, W)","(H, W, N)","(3, N)"],0,"PyTorch convolution layers conventionally use NCHW layout."],["What is the key goal of a validation split?",["Estimate generalization while model choices are being made","Increase the training set to 100 percent","Replace the need for a test set"],0,"Held-out validation data guides development without directly fitting model parameters."]],"x":{"p":"Implement pure-Python `conv_output_size(size, kernel, stride=1, padding=0, dilation=1)` using the standard convolution output formula and floor. Raise ValueError if the result is not positive or any parameter is invalid.","s":"def conv_output_size(size, kernel, stride=1, padding=0, dilation=1):\n    pass\n","h":"Use `(size + 2*padding - dilation*(kernel - 1) - 1) // stride + 1` after validating positive size/kernel/stride/dilation and non-negative padding.","t":"assert conv_output_size(64,3,1,1)==64,\"same-size 3x3 convolution with padding 1\"\nassert conv_output_size(64,3,2,1)==32,\"stride two halves this size\"\nassert conv_output_size(28,5,1,0)==24,\"valid convolution output\""},"d":{"p":"This convolution output formula is missing padding and dilation terms. Implement the standard integer formula and validate the inputs.","s":"def conv_output_size(size, kernel, stride=1, padding=0, dilation=1):\n    return (size - kernel) // stride + 1\n","h":"Use (size + 2*padding - dilation*(kernel-1) - 1) // stride + 1. Size, kernel, stride and dilation are positive; padding is non-negative.","t":"assert conv_output_size(64,3,1,1)==64,\"same spatial size with padding 1\"\nassert conv_output_size(64,3,2,1)==32,\"stride two\"\nassert conv_output_size(28,5,1,0)==24,\"valid convolution\"\ntry: conv_output_size(10,3,0)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"invalid stride should fail\")"},"p":{"n":"Applied project: PyTorch 5: CNNs, Transfer Learning & Reproducibility","p":"Implement `training_step_order()` returning the correct sequence of strings for one PyTorch training iteration. Include `zero_grad`, `forward`, `loss`, `backward`, `step` in the correct order.","s":"def training_step_order():\n    pass\n","h":"Gradients are cleared before the forward pass; the loss is computed before backward; only then can the optimizer step update parameters.","t":"assert training_step_order()==[\"zero_grad\",\"forward\",\"loss\",\"backward\",\"step\"],\"training step order matters\""}}]};
